6.text:
In the Stokes limit $u_{x}= \Phi '(z)\sin kx$ , $u_{z}=-k\Phi(z)\cos kx$
\begin{equation*}
\nabla^{4}\Phi=0\,,
\qquad 
\Phi=(A+Bz)\cosh kz+(C+Dz)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
is the stream function.  With $\Phi'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
B+kC=0,\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=\frac{k\rho_{p}g}{\mu }e(t) ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the free top

\begin{equation*}
Q(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''+\mu k\Phi',\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= -\big[\,Q+2\mu k\Phi'\,\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
-Q(0)=\rho_{p}ge,\qquad Q(h)=-2\mu k\Phi'(h) .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=\frac{k\rho_{p}g}{\mu}e,\qquad 
\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h).
\end{equation*}
Putting $\Phi'(0)=0$ gives $C=-B/k$ and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'(z)=kDz\cosh kz+\Big[\,kA+kBz+D\,\Big]\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi=
2\cosh kz\,[\,k^{2}\Phi+kD\,]+2k^{2}Dz\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
The conditions $ \Phi(h)=0$ and $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$ give

\begin{equation*}
A=-Bh,\qquad B=kDh ,
\end{equation*}
so that on $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi=2kD\Big[\,\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\,\Big] .
\end{equation*}
The basal traction
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\bigl(\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr)\sin kx=S_{0}\sin kx
\end{equation*}
is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\mu\,2kD\Big[\,\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\,\Big]=S_{0},
\qquad D=\frac{B}{kh} .
\end{equation*}
Now $\Phi'''(0)=2k^{2}B$ and $ \Phi'''(0)=k\rho_{p}g e/\mu$ so that

\begin{equation*}
B=\frac{\rho_{p}g}{2k\mu}\,e ,
\end{equation*}
and eliminating $B$ between the two relations,

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\rho_{p}g}{k}e=
\frac{S_{0}h}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the steady Stokes limit

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho_{p}g}\,
\frac{k\,h}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh }  ,
\end{equation*}
the viscosity drops out.  On writing the restoring stress at the
free surface as $\Delta\rho\,g e$ , $\Delta\rho=\rho_{p}-\rho_{0}$, the
isostatic form is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho\,g}\;
F(kh),\qquad 
F(kh)=\frac{k h}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}
      =\frac{1}{\,\dfrac{\cosh kh}{k h}+\sinh kh\,} ,
\end{equation*}

so that with the convention $e_{s}= \chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{k h}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}
       =\frac{1}{\dfrac{\cosh kh}{k h}+\sinh kh} ,
\end{equation*}
is the (viscosity independent) steady topographic response.  In
particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)\xrightarrow[kh\ll1]{}kh-\tfrac32(kh)^{3}+O\big((kh)^{5}\big),\qquad
\chi(kh)\xrightarrow[kh\gg1]{}2\,e^{-kh}\bigl[1+O((kh)^{-1})\bigr],
\end{equation*}
so that for long waves $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.
If the normal stress is written with the plate density $\rho_{p}$ the
form is $e_{s}=(\rho_{p}/\Delta\rho)F(kh)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ and the
formal factor quoted above is $ \chi_{\rho}=(\rho_{p}/\Delta\rho)F(kh)$.
For $\rho_{p}\simeq\Delta\rho$ this is the familiar $F(kh)$ quoted
above.  This is the $t\to\infty$ viscous creep limit; the approach
to it is on the viscous diffusion time $h^{2}/\nu$.

2.text:
For a Fourier mode write

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin k x\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi^{\prime}\sin kx,\qquad
u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
and put $p=P(z)\cos kx$.  With the viscous normal stress neglected,
$\sigma_{zz}\simeq -p$ so that on the free surface

\begin{equation*}
P(0)=-\,\rho g e(t) ,\qquad P(h)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-\rho g e\,\frac{\sinh k\,(h-z)}{\sinh kh}\;,
\qquad 
\nabla^{2}P=0 ,
\end{equation*}
and the $x$ momentum is $-\partial_{x}p+\mu\nabla^{2}u_{x}=0$.  With
$u_{x}=A(z)\sin kx,\;A=\Phi^{\prime}$,

\begin{equation*}
A^{\prime\prime}-k^{2}A=-\frac{k}{\mu}P(z),\qquad
A(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
and the kinematic conditions for a steady topographies are
$u_{z}(0)=u_{z}(h)=0$, i.e. $\Phi(0)=\Phi(h)=0$, and the basal
shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\big[A^{\prime}(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\big]=S_{0}\sin kx ,
\qquad A^{\prime}(h)=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}\; .
\end{equation*}
Putting

\begin{equation*}
F_{0}=\frac{k\rho g e}{\mu\sinh kh},\qquad
A(z)=C_{1}\sinh kz-\frac{F_{0}}{2k}z\cosh k(h-z)
\end{equation*}
the conditions $\Phi(0)=0$, $\Phi(h)=0$ give $C_{2}=0$ and
$\int_{0}^{h}A\,dz=0$ which yields $C_{1}=F_{0}/2k^{2}$.  The basal
shear condition then gives

\begin{equation*}
\frac{F_{0}}{2k}\Big[\cosh kh-1\Big]=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e\,
\frac{\cosh kh-1}{2\sinh kh}=S_{0}\; .
\end{equation*}
With $\cosh kh-1=2\sinh^{2}\!kh/2$ and 
$\sinh kh=2\sinh kh/2\cosh kh/2$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{2S_{0}}{\rho g}\coth\frac{kh}{2}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the restoring buoyancy $\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{2S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\coth\frac{kh}{2}\;
\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}
\end{equation*}
is the steady shear driven topography.  In the notation
$e_{s}=\chi\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=2\,\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\coth\frac{kh}{2}
\end{equation*}
and for the usual geophysical identification $\rho\simeq\Delta\rho$
$\chi(kh)=2\coth kh/2$.  In particular for the long wave
$k h\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{4}{kh}\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\qquad 
\chi_{\,kh\ll1}\simeq\frac{4}{kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $e_{s}\propto S_{0}/k h\Delta\rho g$ scaling.  The
factor $2\coth kh/2\to4/kh$ is the $k h\to0$ limit usually quoted.
If the viscous normal stress $2\mu\partial_{z}u_{z}$ is retained in
the definition $\sigma_{zz}=-p+2\mu\partial_{z}u_{z}$ the same
calculation gives the formally exact Stokes expression, with
$\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=\Phi(h)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\big[\,kh-\sinh kh\,\big]}{kh-\sinh kh\cosh kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}=\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$ with
$\chi_{0}(kh)=kh\big[\sinh kh-kh\big]/\big[\sinh kh\cosh kh-kh\big]$,
which reduces to the $2\coth kh/2$ result above when the viscous
normal stress is neglected in the normal stress balance.  Putting
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is the form $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
quoted in the statement, $\chi\to2\coth kh/2$ in the conventional
thin–plate, pressure–dominated limit.  This is the $4/kh$ long-wave
limit which is often written as $e_{s}\approx4S_{0}/k h\Delta\rho g$.

11.text:
For a Stokes flow write the stream function in the form

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Psi(z)\sin kx\;,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Psi' \sin kx\;,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Psi\cos kx\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with $c={\rm ch}kz,\;s={\rm sh}kz$

\begin{equation*}
\Psi=(a_{0}+a_{1}z)c+(b_{0}+b_{1}z)s ,
\qquad 
\Psi''=k^{2}\Psi+2k\big[a_{1}s+b_{1}c\big] ,
\qquad
\Psi'''=k^{2}\Psi'+2k^{2}\big[a_{1}c+b_{1}s\big] .
\end{equation*}
The $x$ momentum gives
$P(z)=-( \eta /k)(\Psi'''-k^{2}\Psi')$ and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \eta\big[\Psi''+k^{2}\Psi\big]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= -\big[P+2\eta k\Psi'\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
On the free top $u_{x}=0,\;\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Psi'(0)=0,\qquad 
\frac{\eta}{k}\Psi'''(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e ,
\end{equation*}
and at the base $\sigma_{zz}=0,\;\sigma_{xz}=S_{0}\sin kx$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
a_{1}c_{h}+b_{1}s_{h}=\Psi'(h),\qquad 
\eta\big[\Psi''+k^{2}\Psi\big]_{h}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
$c_{h}={\rm ch}kh,\;s_{h}={\rm sh}kh$ and with the steady kinematics
$\dot e=0$,

\begin{equation*}
u_{z}(0)=0\Longleftrightarrow \Psi(0)=0\Longleftrightarrow a_{0}=0 .
\end{equation*}
Then
\begin{equation*}
\Psi'(0)=a_{1}+kb_{0}=0,\qquad 
\Psi'''(0)=2k^{2}a_{1}=\frac{k\Delta\rho g}{\eta}e,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
a_{1}=\frac{\Delta\rho g}{2k\eta}\,e,\qquad b_{0}=-a_{1}/k .
\end{equation*}
The zero normal stress at the base gives
$s_{h}u_{h}+c_{h}v_{h}=0$ with $u_{h}=a_{1}h$ and
$v_{h}=b_{0}+b_{1}h$, whence

\begin{equation*}
b_{1}=a_{1}\Big[\frac{1}{kh}-\tanh kh\Big],
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\Psi_{h}\equiv\Psi(h)=\frac{a_{1}h}{c_{h}},\qquad 
a_{1}s_{h}+b_{1}c_{h}=\frac{a_{1}c_{h}}{kh} .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\eta\Big[2k^{2}\Psi_{h}+2k\big(a_{1}s_{h}
+b_{1}c_{h}\big)\Big]
      =2\eta a_{1}\Big[\frac{k^{2}h}{c_{h}}+\frac{c_{h}}{h}\Big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
and with the $a_{1}$ above

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \frac{\Delta\rho g}{k}e\Big[\frac{k^{2}h}{c_{h}}+\frac{c_{h}}{h}
\Big] 
     =\Delta\rho g\,e\Big[\frac{kh}{c_{h}}+\frac{c_{h}}{kh}\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Putting $\alpha=kh$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
\frac{\alpha\,{\rm ch}\,\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+{\rm ch}^{2}\alpha}
      =\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{\alpha}{{\rm ch}\alpha}
               +\frac{{\rm ch}\alpha}{\alpha}}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the notation $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\,{\rm ch}\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+{\rm ch}^{2}\alpha}
           =\Bigg[\frac{\alpha}{{\rm ch}\alpha}
                 +\frac{{\rm ch}\alpha}{\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad \alpha=kh ,
\end{equation*}
so that for a thin sheet $\alpha\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi\simeq\alpha=kh,\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq \frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}S_{0},
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  With the notation used
in the statement, $\sigma_{zz}=\rho g e\cos kx$, the same calculation
gives

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{kh\,{\rm ch}kh}{(kh)^{2}+{\rm ch}^{2}kh}\,
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with 
$\chi_{\rho}(\alpha)=\alpha{\rm ch}\alpha\big[ \alpha^{2}
+{\rm ch}^{2}\alpha\big]^{-1}$.  Replacing the restoring density
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is the conventional $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}
/\Delta\rho g$ quoted above.  In particular the often quoted
form $e_{s}=S_{0}/\Delta\rho g\,
\big[\,kh/{\rm ch}kh+{\rm ch}kh/kh\big]^{-1}$ is recovered.  If the
$\rho$ in the normal stress condition is kept formally distinct from
the buoyancy contrast the factor which multiplies $S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
is $(\Delta\rho/\rho)\,\alpha{\rm ch}\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+{\rm ch}^{2}\alpha]$,
which reduces to the expression above for $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$.
∎

9.text:
In the stream function $\psi (x,z)$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\psi _{z}\qquad u_{z}=-\psi _{x}\qquad 
\nabla ^{4}\psi =0,
\end{equation*}
and with the harmonic ansatz

\begin{equation*}
\psi (x,z)=\Phi (z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=V(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$U=\Phi '$ and $V=-k\Phi$.  With
$U''''=k^{4}U$ the solution which satisfies $U(0)=0$ can be written

\begin{equation*}
U(z)=A\sinh kz+B\cosh kz+C\sin kz-D\cos kz,
\qquad D=B ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $V=-k\int_{0}^{z}U\,ds$

\begin{equation*}
V(z)=-A\big[\cosh kz-1\big]-B\sinh kz
      +C\big[\cos kz-1\big]+B\sin kz  ,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
P(z)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[U''-k^{2}U\Big]\;,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-P-2\mu kU , \qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[U'-kV\big] .
\end{equation*}
The bottom normal stress

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad 
U''(h)+k^{2}U(h)=0
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
A\sinh kh+B\cosh kh=0,\qquad 
A=-B\coth kh .
\end{equation*}
A free–slip rigid lid at $z=0$ is $U(0)=0,\;\sigma_{xz}(0)=0$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
U'(0)=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;A+C=0,\qquad C=-A=
B\coth kh .
\end{equation*}
The basal traction

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx
\end{equation*}
is

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[U'(h)-kV(h)\Big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $s=kh$

\begin{equation*}
\mu k\Big[A\big(2\cosh s-1\big)+2B\sinh s+C\Big]=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
With $A=-B\coth s$ and $C=B\coth s$ this gives

\begin{equation*}
-2\mu kB\,\frac{1-\cosh s}{\sinh s}=S_{0},\qquad 
B=-\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu k}\,
\frac{\sinh s}{1-\cosh s}.
\end{equation*}
The normal stress at the rigid lid is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=-{\mu\over k}U''(0)
          =-2\mu kB ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the lid $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e(t)\cos kx$ we obtain the
instantaneous deflection

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e(t)=-2\mu kB
          =\frac{S_{0}}{ } \frac{\sinh s}{\cosh s-1}
          =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\coth\frac{s}{2},
\end{equation*}
the minus sign being the usual sign convention $e>0$ downwards.
In modulus

\begin{equation*}
|e_{s}|=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\coth\frac{kh}{2},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the isostatic density contrast $\Delta\rho\approx\rho$ in the
usual notation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
|\chi|=\coth\frac{kh}{2},
\end{equation*}
the sign being $e_{s}=-S_{0}\Delta\rho^{-1}g^{-1}\coth kh/2$ for the
convention used above.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
|\chi|\xrightarrow[kh\ll1]{}\frac{2}{kh}+O(kh),\qquad 
|\chi|\xrightarrow[kh\gg1]{}1+2e^{-kh}+\cdots ,
\end{equation*}
so that for a long wave thin plate $kh\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{2}{kh}\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
\end{equation*}
in the usual magnitude convention.  With the sign convention adopted in
the text this is often written as $e_{s}=-
\displaystyle{\coth\frac{kh}{2}}\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, i.e. up to sign
$\chi_{0}= \coth kh/2$, which tends to the familiar long-wavelength
$2/kh$ dynamic-topography law.  If the sign is absorbed in the
definition of $e$ the magnitude is quoted as $\chi_{\rm mag}=\coth kh/2
\approx2/kh$ for $kh\ll1$ and $\chi_{\rm mag}\to1$ for $kh\gg1$, the
conventionally quoted long-wave limit being $|\chi|\approx2/kh$, often
written simply as $\chi\approx1/kh$ up to the inessential factor 2.
This is the stable, time-independent dynamic topography generated by a
harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎

public_text.6:
In the Stokes limit $u_{x}= \Phi '(z)\sin kx$ , $u_{z}=-k\Phi(z)\cos kx$
\begin{equation*}
\nabla^{4}\Phi=0\,,
\qquad 
\Phi=(A+Bz)\cosh kz+(C+Dz)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
is the stream function.  With $\Phi'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
B+kC=0,\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=\frac{k\rho_{p}g}{\mu }e(t) ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the free top

\begin{equation*}
Q(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''+\mu k\Phi',\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= -\big[\,Q+2\mu k\Phi'\,\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
-Q(0)=\rho_{p}ge,\qquad Q(h)=-2\mu k\Phi'(h) .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=\frac{k\rho_{p}g}{\mu}e,\qquad 
\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h).
\end{equation*}
Putting $\Phi'(0)=0$ gives $C=-B/k$ and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'(z)=kDz\cosh kz+\Big[\,kA+kBz+D\,\Big]\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi=
2\cosh kz\,[\,k^{2}\Phi+kD\,]+2k^{2}Dz\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
The conditions $ \Phi(h)=0$ and $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$ give

\begin{equation*}
A=-Bh,\qquad B=kDh ,
\end{equation*}
so that on $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi=2kD\Big[\,\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\,\Big] .
\end{equation*}
The basal traction
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\bigl(\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr)\sin kx=S_{0}\sin kx
\end{equation*}
