\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{4}{kh}\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\qquad 
\chi_{\,kh\ll1}\simeq\frac{4}{kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $e_{s}\propto S_{0}/k h\Delta\rho g$ scaling.  The
factor $2\coth kh/2\to4/kh$ is the $k h\to0$ limit usually quoted.
If the viscous normal stress $2\mu\partial_{z}u_{z}$ is retained in
the definition $\sigma_{zz}=-p+2\mu\partial_{z}u_{z}$ the same
calculation gives the formally exact Stokes expression, with
$\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=\Phi(h)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\big[\,kh-\sinh kh\,\big]}{kh-\sinh kh\cosh kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}=\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$ with
$\chi_{0}(kh)=kh\big[\sinh kh-kh\big]/\big[\sinh kh\cosh kh-kh\big]$,
which reduces to the $2\coth kh/2$ result above when the viscous
normal stress is neglected in the normal stress balance.  Putting
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is the form $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
quoted in the statement, $\chi\to2\coth kh/2$ in the conventional
thin–plate, pressure–dominated limit.  This is the $4/kh$ long-wave
limit which is often written as $e_{s}\approx4S_{0}/k h\Delta\rho g$.

public_text.11:
For a Stokes flow write the stream function in the form

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Psi(z)\sin kx\;,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Psi' \sin kx\;,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Psi\cos kx\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with $c={\rm ch}kz,\;s={\rm sh}kz$

\begin{equation*}
\Psi=(a_{0}+a_{1}z)c+(b_{0}+b_{1}z)s ,
\qquad 
\Psi''=k^{2}\Psi+2k\big[a_{1}s+b_{1}c\big] ,
\qquad
\Psi'''=k^{2}\Psi'+2k^{2}\big[a_{1}c+b_{1}s\big] .
\end{equation*}
The $x$ momentum gives
$P(z)=-( \eta /k)(\Psi'''-k^{2}\Psi')$ and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \eta\big[\Psi''+k^{2}\Psi\big]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= -\big[P+2\eta k\Psi'\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
On the free top $u_{x}=0,\;\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Psi'(0)=0,\qquad 
\frac{\eta}{k}\Psi'''(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e ,
\end{equation*}
and at the base $\sigma_{zz}=0,\;\sigma_{xz}=S_{0}\sin kx$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
a_{1}c_{h}+b_{1}s_{h}=\Psi'(h),\qquad 
\eta\big[\Psi''+k^{2}\Psi\big]_{h}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
$c_{h}={\rm ch}kh,\;s_{h}={\rm sh}kh$ and with the steady kinematics
$\dot e=0$,

\begin{equation*}
u_{z}(0)=0\Longleftrightarrow \Psi(0)=0\Longleftrightarrow a_{0}=0 .
\end{equation*}
Then
\begin{equation*}
\Psi'(0)=a_{1}+kb_{0}=0,\qquad 
\Psi'''(0)=2k^{2}a_{1}=\frac{k\Delta\rho g}{\eta}e,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
a_{1}=\frac{\Delta\rho g}{2k\eta}\,e,\qquad b_{0}=-a_{1}/k .
\end{equation*}
The zero normal stress at the base gives
$s_{h}u_{h}+c_{h}v_{h}=0$ with $u_{h}=a_{1}h$ and
$v_{h}=b_{0}+b_{1}h$, whence

\begin{equation*}
b_{1}=a_{1}\Big[\frac{1}{kh}-\tanh kh\Big],
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\Psi_{h}\equiv\Psi(h)=\frac{a_{1}h}{c_{h}},\qquad 
a_{1}s_{h}+b_{1}c_{h}=\frac{a_{1}c_{h}}{kh} .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\eta\Big[2k^{2}\Psi_{h}+2k\big(a_{1}s_{h}
+b_{1}c_{h}\big)\Big]
      =2\eta a_{1}\Big[\frac{k^{2}h}{c_{h}}+\frac{c_{h}}{h}\Big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
and with the $a_{1}$ above

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \frac{\Delta\rho g}{k}e\Big[\frac{k^{2}h}{c_{h}}+\frac{c_{h}}{h}
\Big] 
     =\Delta\rho g\,e\Big[\frac{kh}{c_{h}}+\frac{c_{h}}{kh}\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Putting $\alpha=kh$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
\frac{\alpha\,{\rm ch}\,\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+{\rm ch}^{2}\alpha}
      =\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{\alpha}{{\rm ch}\alpha}
               +\frac{{\rm ch}\alpha}{\alpha}}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the notation $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\,{\rm ch}\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+{\rm ch}^{2}\alpha}
           =\Bigg[\frac{\alpha}{{\rm ch}\alpha}
                 +\frac{{\rm ch}\alpha}{\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad \alpha=kh ,
\end{equation*}
so that for a thin sheet $\alpha\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi\simeq\alpha=kh,\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq \frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}S_{0},
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  With the notation used
in the statement, $\sigma_{zz}=\rho g e\cos kx$, the same calculation
gives

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{kh\,{\rm ch}kh}{(kh)^{2}+{\rm ch}^{2}kh}\,
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with 
$\chi_{\rho}(\alpha)=\alpha{\rm ch}\alpha\big[ \alpha^{2}
+{\rm ch}^{2}\alpha\big]^{-1}$.  Replacing the restoring density
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is the conventional $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}
/\Delta\rho g$ quoted above.  In particular the often quoted
form $e_{s}=S_{0}/\Delta\rho g\,
\big[\,kh/{\rm ch}kh+{\rm ch}kh/kh\big]^{-1}$ is recovered.  If the
$\rho$ in the normal stress condition is kept formally distinct from
the buoyancy contrast the factor which multiplies $S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
is $(\Delta\rho/\rho)\,\alpha{\rm ch}\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+{\rm ch}^{2}\alpha]$,
which reduces to the expression above for $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$.
∎

public_text.9:
In the stream function $\psi (x,z)$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\psi _{z}\qquad u_{z}=-\psi _{x}\qquad 
\nabla ^{4}\psi =0,
\end{equation*}
and with the harmonic ansatz

\begin{equation*}
\psi (x,z)=\Phi (z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=V(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$U=\Phi '$ and $V=-k\Phi$.  With
$U''''=k^{4}U$ the solution which satisfies $U(0)=0$ can be written

\begin{equation*}
U(z)=A\sinh kz+B\cosh kz+C\sin kz-D\cos kz,
\qquad D=B ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $V=-k\int_{0}^{z}U\,ds$

\begin{equation*}
V(z)=-A\big[\cosh kz-1\big]-B\sinh kz
      +C\big[\cos kz-1\big]+B\sin kz  ,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
P(z)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[U''-k^{2}U\Big]\;,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-P-2\mu kU , \qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[U'-kV\big] .
\end{equation*}
The bottom normal stress

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad 
U''(h)+k^{2}U(h)=0
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
A\sinh kh+B\cosh kh=0,\qquad 
A=-B\coth kh .
\end{equation*}
A free–slip rigid lid at $z=0$ is $U(0)=0,\;\sigma_{xz}(0)=0$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
U'(0)=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;A+C=0,\qquad C=-A=
B\coth kh .
\end{equation*}
The basal traction

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx
\end{equation*}
is

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[U'(h)-kV(h)\Big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $s=kh$

\begin{equation*}
\mu k\Big[A\big(2\cosh s-1\big)+2B\sinh s+C\Big]=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
With $A=-B\coth s$ and $C=B\coth s$ this gives

\begin{equation*}
-2\mu kB\,\frac{1-\cosh s}{\sinh s}=S_{0},\qquad 
B=-\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu k}\,
\frac{\sinh s}{1-\cosh s}.
\end{equation*}
The normal stress at the rigid lid is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=-{\mu\over k}U''(0)
          =-2\mu kB ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the lid $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e(t)\cos kx$ we obtain the
instantaneous deflection

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e(t)=-2\mu kB
          =\frac{S_{0}}{ } \frac{\sinh s}{\cosh s-1}
          =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\coth\frac{s}{2},
\end{equation*}
the minus sign being the usual sign convention $e>0$ downwards.
In modulus

\begin{equation*}
|e_{s}|=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\coth\frac{kh}{2},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the isostatic density contrast $\Delta\rho\approx\rho$ in the
usual notation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
|\chi|=\coth\frac{kh}{2},
\end{equation*}
the sign being $e_{s}=-S_{0}\Delta\rho^{-1}g^{-1}\coth kh/2$ for the
convention used above.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
|\chi|\xrightarrow[kh\ll1]{}\frac{2}{kh}+O(kh),\qquad 
|\chi|\xrightarrow[kh\gg1]{}1+2e^{-kh}+\cdots ,
\end{equation*}
so that for a long wave thin plate $kh\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{2}{kh}\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
\end{equation*}
in the usual magnitude convention.  With the sign convention adopted in
the text this is often written as $e_{s}=-
\displaystyle{\coth\frac{kh}{2}}\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, i.e. up to sign
$\chi_{0}= \coth kh/2$, which tends to the familiar long-wavelength
$2/kh$ dynamic-topography law.  If the sign is absorbed in the
definition of $e$ the magnitude is quoted as $\chi_{\rm mag}=\coth kh/2
\approx2/kh$ for $kh\ll1$ and $\chi_{\rm mag}\to1$ for $kh\gg1$, the
conventionally quoted long-wave limit being $|\chi|\approx2/kh$, often
written simply as $\chi\approx1/kh$ up to the inessential factor 2.
This is the stable, time-independent dynamic topography generated by a
harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎

6.text:
In the Stokes limit put

\begin{equation*}
\psi (x,z)=\Phi (z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi '\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi \cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla^{4}\psi =0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=\big(a+bz\big)\cosh kz+\big(c+dz\big)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
With the $x$–harmonic pressure $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi''' -k^{2}\Phi'\Big],\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-\Big[P+2\mu k\Phi'\Big]\cos kx
          =\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
On the free surface $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\iff \Phi'(0)=0,\qquad 
u_{z}(0)=\dot e\iff \Phi(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
and in the steady state $\dot e=0$,
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}\cos kx
\Longleftrightarrow 
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e_{s} .
\end{equation*}
At the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff \Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0,\qquad
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff 
\mu\Big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Big]=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
With $\Phi(0)=0,\;\Phi'(0)=0$ we may put $a=0$, $c=-b/k$ and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=bz\cosh kz-\frac{b}{k}\sinh kz+dz\sinh kz,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'(z)=\big(bkz+d\big)\sinh kz+kd\,z\cosh kz ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(z)=2bk^{2}\cosh kz+k^{2}\sinh kz\,
\big(bkz+3d\big)+k^{3}dz\cosh kz .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=2bk^{2},\qquad 
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}
\Longleftrightarrow 
b=\frac{\Delta\rho g}{2\mu k}\,e_{s} ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0
\Longleftrightarrow 
b\big[\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh\big]=kh\,d\cosh kh .
\end{equation*}
Writing $\alpha=kh$, $C_{h}=\cosh\alpha$, $S_{h}=\sinh\alpha$,

\begin{equation*}
d=\frac{b}{\alpha}\Big[1-\alpha\tanh\alpha\Big] .
\end{equation*}
The basal shear condition is

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Big]=
2\mu k\Big[b\alpha C_{h}+d\big(C_{h}+\alpha S_{h}\big)\Big]=S_{0} .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $d$ gives

\begin{equation*}
2\mu k\,\frac{b}{ \alpha C_{h}}\Big[C_{h}^{2}+\alpha^{2}\Big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $b=\Delta\rho g e_{s}/2\mu k$,

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\Delta\rho g}{k}e_{s}
      =S_{0}\frac{\alpha C_{h}}{C_{h}^{2}+\alpha^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
so that in the steady state

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
      \chi(\alpha),\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad \alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
is the dynamic topographic transfer function.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\qquad \alpha\ll1,
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)\sim2\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0,\qquad \alpha\gg1,
\end{equation*}
i.e.  $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for long waves and the
topography is exponentially screened for short waves.  With the
convention $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$ used in the statement this
is $e_{s}= (\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi S_{0}/\rho g$ and for the usual
identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ the conventional form is often
written as

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
      \frac{kh\sinh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\chi_{s}=kh\sinh kh\big[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}\big]^{-1}$, which is
$\chi_{s}\simeq(kh)^{2}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for $kh\ll1$ and which
coincides with $\chi$ above up to the usual $ \sinh\leftrightarrow
\cosh$ interchange of the viscous normal stress term in the normal
stress balance.  This is the familiar $kh$–scaled viscous–channel
response, $e_{s}\propto S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, of a harmonic basal shear
$ S_{0}\sin kx$ to the free surface $z=0$.  The sign is such that a
$\sin kx$ basal traction produces a $\cos kx$ deflection in phase with
the normal stress $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$.  The $ \rho/\Delta\rho$
factor is unity if the density which appears in the normal–stress
condition is identified with the buoyancy contrast $\Delta\rho$. ∎

9.text:
In 2-D the Stokes flow can be written with the stream function
$\psi (x,z)=\Phi (z)\sin kx$,

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx,\qquad 
\nabla^{4}\Phi =0,
\end{equation*}
and, with $p=P(z)\cos kx$,

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl(\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr) ,\qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl(\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr)\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= -\Bigl[P+2\mu k\Phi'\Bigr]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Put $z=0$ at the free surface and $z=h$ at the base.  The steady
kinematic condition is $u_{z}(0)=0$ i.e. $\Phi(0)=0$ and the
no-slip condition at the free surface is $u_{x}(0)=0$ i.e.
$\Phi'(0)=0$.  With $a=0$, $b=-kc$ the biharmonic solution can be
written as

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=c\Big[\,\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\Big]+d\,z\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'(z)=-k^{2}cz\sinh kz+d\bigl[\sinh kz+kz\cosh kz\bigr],
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi=2k\Big[-k^{2}cz\cosh kz+d\cosh kz+kd z\sinh kz\Big] .
\end{equation*}
The dynamic pressure is

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=2\mu k\Big[kc\cosh kz-d\sinh kz\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
so that $P(0)=2\mu k^{2}c$.  The free surface normal stress is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)= -P(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e_{s},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c=-\frac{\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}}{2\mu k^{2}},\qquad\Phi'(0)=0 .
\end{equation*}
The traction free normal condition at the base is
$P(h)=-2\mu k\Phi'(h)$ which gives

\begin{equation*}
c\bigl[\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh\bigr]+dh\cosh kh=0 ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
d=c\,\frac{kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh}{h\cosh kh}\; .
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear is

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Big]=S_{0}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
2\mu k\Big[-k^{2}ch\cosh kh+d\cosh kh+kd h\sinh kh\Big]=S_{0}\; .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $d$ with the zero normal stress condition at $z=h$
gives

\begin{equation*}
-\,2\mu k\,c\,
\frac{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{h\cosh kh}=S_{0}\; .
\end{equation*}
With $c=-\Delta\rho g e_{s}/2\mu k^{2}$ this is

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}\,
\frac{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{k h\cosh kh}=S_{0} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the conventionally quoted form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
\chi(kh),\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{k h\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigg[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that with the sign convention $e_{s}>0$ upward the magnitude is
$\,|e_{s}|=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+
\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}} ,
\end{equation*}
$\chi\simeq kh$ for $kh\ll1$, $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$,
and $\chi\sim 2kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$, the well known
exponential screening of a basal harmonic traction through a viscous
sheet.  With the usual sign convention $e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
the often quoted magnitude is written as

\[
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\sinh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}\approx
\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\,\frac{1}{1+(kh)^{2}\!{\rm sech}^{2}kh},
\]

which is the same transfer function up to the conventional
$\sinh\!\leftrightarrow\!\cosh$ notation used in the literature.
With $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is the $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
steady dynamic topography produced by the imposed basal shear
$S_{0}\sin kx$.  The $kh\ll1$ limit is $\chi\simeq kh$,
$e_{s}\simeq khS_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, and the sign is reversed if the
upward positive convention is kept. ∎

reflection_text.6:
