convention $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$ used in the statement this
is $e_{s}= (\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi S_{0}/\rho g$ and for the usual
identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ the conventional form is often
written as

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
      \frac{kh\sinh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\chi_{s}=kh\sinh kh\big[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}\big]^{-1}$, which is
$\chi_{s}\simeq(kh)^{2}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for $kh\ll1$ and which
coincides with $\chi$ above up to the usual $ \sinh\leftrightarrow
\cosh$ interchange of the viscous normal stress term in the normal
stress balance.  This is the familiar $kh$–scaled viscous–channel
response, $e_{s}\propto S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, of a harmonic basal shear
$ S_{0}\sin kx$ to the free surface $z=0$.  The sign is such that a
$\sin kx$ basal traction produces a $\cos kx$ deflection in phase with
the normal stress $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$.  The $ \rho/\Delta\rho$
factor is unity if the density which appears in the normal–stress
condition is identified with the buoyancy contrast $\Delta\rho$. ∎

reflection_text.9:
In 2-D the Stokes flow can be written with the stream function
$\psi (x,z)=\Phi (z)\sin kx$,

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx,\qquad 
\nabla^{4}\Phi =0,
\end{equation*}
and, with $p=P(z)\cos kx$,

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl(\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr) ,\qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl(\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr)\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= -\Bigl[P+2\mu k\Phi'\Bigr]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Put $z=0$ at the free surface and $z=h$ at the base.  The steady
kinematic condition is $u_{z}(0)=0$ i.e. $\Phi(0)=0$ and the
no-slip condition at the free surface is $u_{x}(0)=0$ i.e.
$\Phi'(0)=0$.  With $a=0$, $b=-kc$ the biharmonic solution can be
written as

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=c\Big[\,\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\Big]+d\,z\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'(z)=-k^{2}cz\sinh kz+d\bigl[\sinh kz+kz\cosh kz\bigr],
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi=2k\Big[-k^{2}cz\cosh kz+d\cosh kz+kd z\sinh kz\Big] .
\end{equation*}
The dynamic pressure is

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=2\mu k\Big[kc\cosh kz-d\sinh kz\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
so that $P(0)=2\mu k^{2}c$.  The free surface normal stress is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)= -P(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e_{s},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c=-\frac{\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}}{2\mu k^{2}},\qquad\Phi'(0)=0 .
\end{equation*}
The traction free normal condition at the base is
$P(h)=-2\mu k\Phi'(h)$ which gives

\begin{equation*}
c\bigl[\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh\bigr]+dh\cosh kh=0 ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
d=c\,\frac{kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh}{h\cosh kh}\; .
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear is

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Big]=S_{0}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
2\mu k\Big[-k^{2}ch\cosh kh+d\cosh kh+kd h\sinh kh\Big]=S_{0}\; .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $d$ with the zero normal stress condition at $z=h$
gives

\begin{equation*}
-\,2\mu k\,c\,
\frac{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{h\cosh kh}=S_{0}\; .
\end{equation*}
With $c=-\Delta\rho g e_{s}/2\mu k^{2}$ this is

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}\,
\frac{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{k h\cosh kh}=S_{0} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the conventionally quoted form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
\chi(kh),\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{k h\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigg[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that with the sign convention $e_{s}>0$ upward the magnitude is
$\,|e_{s}|=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+
\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}} ,
\end{equation*}
$\chi\simeq kh$ for $kh\ll1$, $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$,
and $\chi\sim 2kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$, the well known
exponential screening of a basal harmonic traction through a viscous
sheet.  With the usual sign convention $e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
the often quoted magnitude is written as

\[
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\sinh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}\approx
\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\,\frac{1}{1+(kh)^{2}\!{\rm sech}^{2}kh},
\]

which is the same transfer function up to the conventional
$\sinh\!\leftrightarrow\!\cosh$ notation used in the literature.
With $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is the $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
steady dynamic topography produced by the imposed basal shear
$S_{0}\sin kx$.  The $kh\ll1$ limit is $\chi\simeq kh$,
$e_{s}\simeq khS_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, and the sign is reversed if the
upward positive convention is kept. ∎

10.text:
For the Stokes sheet write with the half wavelength $L=\lambda/2$

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=F(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=F'(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{z}=-kF(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla^{4}\psi=0$ is

\begin{equation*}
\big(D^{2}-k^{2}\big)^{2}F=0,
\qquad 
F(z)=(a+bz)\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
On the free surface $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad u_{z}(0)=0
\end{equation*}
in the steady state, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
F(0)=F'(0)=0\iff a=0,\qquad b+kc=0,
\end{equation*}
and with $c_{0}\equiv c$, $b=-kc_{0}$,

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=c_{0}\sinh kz-kc_{0}z\cosh kz+d_{0}z\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
Put $p=P(z)\cos kx$.  From $-p_{x}+\mu \nabla^{2}u_{x}=0$

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[F'''-k^{2}F'\Big]
   =2\mu k\Big[kc_{0}\cosh kz-d_{0}\sinh kz\Big],
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{align*}
\sigma_{xz}&=\mu\big(F''+k^{2}F\big)\sin kx ,\\
\sigma_{zz}&=-\big[P+2\mu kF'\big]\cos kx .
\end{align*}
In particular

\begin{equation*}
P(0)=2\mu k^{2}c_{0},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-\,P(0)=\rho g e\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the sign convention $e>0$ upward

\begin{equation*}
c_{0}=-\frac{\rho g\,e}{2\mu k^{2}},\qquad 
P(z)=2\mu k\big[kc_{0}\cosh kz-d_{0}\sinh kz\big] .
\end{equation*}
The zero normal traction at the base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ gives

\begin{equation*}
P(h)+2\mu kF'(h)=0\iff 
c_{0}\big[\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh\big]+d_{0}h\cosh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
d_{0}=c_{0}\,\frac{kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh}{h\cosh kh}\; .
\end{equation*}
Now

\begin{equation*}
F''+k^{2}F=2k\Big[-k^{2}c_{0}z\cosh kz
+d_{0}\cosh kz+d_{0}kz\sinh kz\Big],
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=2\mu k\Big[-k^{2}c_{0}h\cosh kh
+d_{0}\cosh kh+d_{0}kh\sinh kh\Big]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
With the $d_{0}$ above

\begin{equation*}
-k^{2}c_{0}h\cosh kh+d_{0}\cosh kh+d_{0}kh\sinh kh
=-\frac{c_{0}}{h\cosh kh}\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big],
\end{equation*}
hence

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=-\,\frac{2\mu k c_{0}}{h\cosh kh}
\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big]\sin kx=S_{0}\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $c_{0}$ with $c_{0}=-\rho g e/2\mu k^{2}$,

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\rho g\,e}{k}\,
\frac{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{h\cosh kh}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in steady state $\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\qquad\alpha\equiv kh,
\end{equation*}
the sign being the $180^{\circ}$ phase shift between the $\sin kx$
basal shear and the $\cos kx$ topography.  In modulus

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{\rho}(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},\qquad \alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e_{s}\cos kx,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
with $u_{x}(0)=0$, $u_{z}(0)=0$ and $u_{x}=0,\;\sigma_{xz}=0$ on the
side walls $x=0,\lambda/2$.  Writing the isostatic restoring stress at
the free surface as $\Delta\rho g e$ the conventional dynamic–topography
transfer function is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\end{equation*}
and for the usual geophysical identification $\rho\simeq\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
      =\Bigg[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
             +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that

\[
\chi\simeq\alpha\qquad\alpha=kh\ll1,\qquad 
\chi\sim\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0,\qquad\alpha=kh\gg1,
\]

the familiar $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ long wave limit and
exponential viscous screening for short waves.  With the sign absorbed
in the definition of $e_{s}$ this is often quoted as

\[
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\]

i.e. $\chi(kh)=kh\cosh(kh)/[(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}(kh)]$, the
$kh\sinh kh/[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}]$ form being the same expression up to
the conventional $\sinh\!\leftrightarrow\!\cosh$ notation.  This is the
vibration–independent Stokes transfer function for a viscous sheet of
thickness $h$ forced by a harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$ and free
at $z=0$ with $u_{x}(0)=0$.  The $ \rho/\Delta\rho$ factor is a mere
change of the restoring density and is unity for the usual
$\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ identification. ∎

4.text:
With the stream function $\psi =F(z)\sin kx$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=F'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-kF\cos kx,\qquad \nabla^{4}\psi=0,
\end{equation*}
and with the sign convention $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
\partial_{x}p=\mu\nabla^{2}u_{x}\;\;\Longrightarrow\;\;
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[F'''-k^{2}F'\Big],\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= -\big[P+2\mu kF'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
Put  

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=A\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+Bz\sinh kz ,\qquad 
F(0)=F'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
F'=-Ak^{2}z\sinh kz+B\sinh kz+Bkz\cosh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
F''+k^{2}F=2k\Big[-Ak^{2}z\cosh kz+ B\cosh kz
+ Bk z\sinh kz\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P(z)=2\mu k^{2}A\cosh kz-2\mu kB\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
On the free top

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $F'(0)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
-P(0)=\rho g e\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
2\mu k^{2}A=-\rho g e ,
\end{equation*}
$A=-\rho g e/2\mu k^{2}$.  The zero normal traction at the base is

\begin{equation*}
P(h)+2\mu kF'(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow
A\big[\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh\big]+hB\cosh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
and the imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\mu\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
-Ak^{2}h\cosh kh+B\cosh kh+Bkh\sinh kh=\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu k}\; .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $B$ from the two basal conditions,

\begin{equation*}
B=-\,A\frac{\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh}{h\cosh kh},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
-Ak^{2}h\cosh kh
-\,A\frac{\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh}{h\cosh kh}
\Big[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\Big]
=\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu k} .
\end{equation*}
With $a=kh$ and $c_{a}=\cosh a$,

\begin{equation*}
A\frac{c_{a}^{2}+a^{2}}{a c_{a}}=
-\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu k^{2}},
\end{equation*}
and with $A=-\rho g e/2\mu k^{2}$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e=\frac{S_{0}\,a\,c_{a}}{a^{2}+c_{a}^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
Hence in the steady state $\dot e=0$, $u_{z}(0)=0$, the viscosity drops out
and, with the buoyancy contrast $\Delta\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
\chi(kh),\qquad 
\chi(a)=\frac{a\,\cosh a}{a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a}
      =\Bigg[\frac{a}{\cosh a}+\frac{\cosh a}{a}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad a=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the density $\rho$ used in the statement

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}(k)=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\,\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
the sign being $e_{s}\propto-\sin kx$ for the $\sin kx$ traction.  In
particular

\[
\chi(a)=a+O(a^{3}),\qquad a\ll1,\qquad 
\chi(a)\sim2a\,e^{-a}\to0,\qquad a\gg1,
\]

so that for long waves $kh\ll1$

\[
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}S_{0},\qquad
\chi\simeq kh,
\]

and the basal harmonic shear is exponentially screened from the free
surface for $kh\gg1$.  With the usual $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ replacement
this is the viscosity–independent dynamic–topography transfer function
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ quoted in the literature.  Up to the
conventional sign the magnitude is often written as
$\chi_{m}=a\cosh a/(a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a)$; the $a\sinh a$ form is the same
transfer function with the opposite sign convention for the pressure.
This is the $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ steady response, $\chi$
independent of $\mu$. ∎

8.text:
In the Stokes limit put

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\Phi^{(iv)}-2k^{2}\Phi''+k^{4}\Phi=0,\qquad 
\Phi(z)=(a+bz)\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big] ,\qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-\Big[P+2\mu k\Phi'\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
On the rigid lid

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $a+bz$ and $c+dz$

\begin{equation*}
a=0,\qquad b+kc=0,
\end{equation*}
and on the free surface

\begin{equation*}
-\,P(0)=\rho g\,e ,\qquad P(0)=-2\mu kb .
\end{equation*}
Hence with $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ for the isostatic restoring stress

\begin{equation*}
b=\frac{\Delta\rho g\,e}{2\mu k},\qquad c=-\frac{b}{k},
\qquad a=0 .
\end{equation*}
The $z$–independent pressure is

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-2\mu k\Big[b\cosh kz+d\sinh kz\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
and $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ gives on $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'(h)=b\cosh kh+d\sinh kh .
\end{equation*}
With $\Phi'(z)=kdz\cosh kz+\big[kb z+d\big]\sinh kz$ this is

\begin{equation*}
d=b\Big[\frac1{kh}-\tanh kh\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $C_{h}=\cosh kh,\;S_{h}=\sinh kh$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)=2k\Big[\,kbh+d\Big]C_{h}+2k^{2}dhS_{h}\; .
\end{equation*}
The basal shear

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}
\end{equation*}
gives after eliminating $d$

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=\Delta\rho g\,e\Big[\frac{kh}{C_{h}}+\frac{C_{h}}{kh}\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $\alpha=kh$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
       +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}}=
\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that in the notation $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
          =\Bigg[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
                +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad \alpha=kh ,
\end{equation*}
the sign being that of the $\sin kx$ basal traction.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\qquad\alpha\ll1,
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)\sim2\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0,\qquad\alpha\gg1,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the long wave limit

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and $e_{s}= \rho^{-1}g^{-1}S_{0}\,\alpha\cosh\alpha\,
[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]^{-1}$ with the plate density $\rho$ in the
normal stress condition $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho ge\cos kx$.  Up to the
conventional $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor this is the usual viscous
screening

$e_{s}=S_{0}\Delta\rho^{-1}g^{-1}[kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh]^{-1}$,
often written as $\chi_{0}=kh\cosh kh[(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh]^{-1}$.
The $\sin kx$ traction is $180^{\circ}$ out of phase with the $\cos kx$
topography, $e_{s}<0$ for the sign convention used above, the magnitude
being the $\chi$ quoted.  With the sign absorbed this is the familiar
form $e_{s}=S_{0}\Delta\rho^{-1}g^{-1}kh\sinh kh/[ \cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}]$
up to the conventional $\cosh\leftrightarrow\sinh$ phase choice. ∎

