side walls $x=0,\lambda/2$.  Writing the isostatic restoring stress at
the free surface as $\Delta\rho g e$ the conventional dynamic–topography
transfer function is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\end{equation*}
and for the usual geophysical identification $\rho\simeq\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
      =\Bigg[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
             +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that

\[
\chi\simeq\alpha\qquad\alpha=kh\ll1,\qquad 
\chi\sim\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0,\qquad\alpha=kh\gg1,
\]

the familiar $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ long wave limit and
exponential viscous screening for short waves.  With the sign absorbed
in the definition of $e_{s}$ this is often quoted as

\[
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\]

i.e. $\chi(kh)=kh\cosh(kh)/[(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}(kh)]$, the
$kh\sinh kh/[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}]$ form being the same expression up to
the conventional $\sinh\!\leftrightarrow\!\cosh$ notation.  This is the
vibration–independent Stokes transfer function for a viscous sheet of
thickness $h$ forced by a harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$ and free
at $z=0$ with $u_{x}(0)=0$.  The $ \rho/\Delta\rho$ factor is a mere
change of the restoring density and is unity for the usual
$\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ identification. ∎

public_text.4:
With the stream function $\psi =F(z)\sin kx$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=F'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-kF\cos kx,\qquad \nabla^{4}\psi=0,
\end{equation*}
and with the sign convention $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
\partial_{x}p=\mu\nabla^{2}u_{x}\;\;\Longrightarrow\;\;
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[F'''-k^{2}F'\Big],\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= -\big[P+2\mu kF'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
Put  

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=A\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+Bz\sinh kz ,\qquad 
F(0)=F'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
F'=-Ak^{2}z\sinh kz+B\sinh kz+Bkz\cosh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
F''+k^{2}F=2k\Big[-Ak^{2}z\cosh kz+ B\cosh kz
+ Bk z\sinh kz\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P(z)=2\mu k^{2}A\cosh kz-2\mu kB\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
On the free top

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $F'(0)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
-P(0)=\rho g e\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
2\mu k^{2}A=-\rho g e ,
\end{equation*}
$A=-\rho g e/2\mu k^{2}$.  The zero normal traction at the base is

\begin{equation*}
P(h)+2\mu kF'(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow
A\big[\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh\big]+hB\cosh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
and the imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\mu\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
-Ak^{2}h\cosh kh+B\cosh kh+Bkh\sinh kh=\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu k}\; .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $B$ from the two basal conditions,

\begin{equation*}
B=-\,A\frac{\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh}{h\cosh kh},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
-Ak^{2}h\cosh kh
-\,A\frac{\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh}{h\cosh kh}
\Big[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\Big]
=\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu k} .
\end{equation*}
With $a=kh$ and $c_{a}=\cosh a$,

\begin{equation*}
A\frac{c_{a}^{2}+a^{2}}{a c_{a}}=
-\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu k^{2}},
\end{equation*}
and with $A=-\rho g e/2\mu k^{2}$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e=\frac{S_{0}\,a\,c_{a}}{a^{2}+c_{a}^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
Hence in the steady state $\dot e=0$, $u_{z}(0)=0$, the viscosity drops out
and, with the buoyancy contrast $\Delta\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
\chi(kh),\qquad 
\chi(a)=\frac{a\,\cosh a}{a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a}
      =\Bigg[\frac{a}{\cosh a}+\frac{\cosh a}{a}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad a=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the density $\rho$ used in the statement

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}(k)=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\,\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
the sign being $e_{s}\propto-\sin kx$ for the $\sin kx$ traction.  In
particular

\[
\chi(a)=a+O(a^{3}),\qquad a\ll1,\qquad 
\chi(a)\sim2a\,e^{-a}\to0,\qquad a\gg1,
\]

so that for long waves $kh\ll1$

\[
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}S_{0},\qquad
\chi\simeq kh,
\]

and the basal harmonic shear is exponentially screened from the free
surface for $kh\gg1$.  With the usual $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ replacement
this is the viscosity–independent dynamic–topography transfer function
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ quoted in the literature.  Up to the
conventional sign the magnitude is often written as
$\chi_{m}=a\cosh a/(a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a)$; the $a\sinh a$ form is the same
transfer function with the opposite sign convention for the pressure.
This is the $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ steady response, $\chi$
independent of $\mu$. ∎

public_text.8:
In the Stokes limit put

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\Phi^{(iv)}-2k^{2}\Phi''+k^{4}\Phi=0,\qquad 
\Phi(z)=(a+bz)\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big] ,\qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-\Big[P+2\mu k\Phi'\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
On the rigid lid

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $a+bz$ and $c+dz$

\begin{equation*}
a=0,\qquad b+kc=0,
\end{equation*}
and on the free surface

\begin{equation*}
-\,P(0)=\rho g\,e ,\qquad P(0)=-2\mu kb .
\end{equation*}
Hence with $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ for the isostatic restoring stress

\begin{equation*}
b=\frac{\Delta\rho g\,e}{2\mu k},\qquad c=-\frac{b}{k},
\qquad a=0 .
\end{equation*}
The $z$–independent pressure is

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-2\mu k\Big[b\cosh kz+d\sinh kz\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
and $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ gives on $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'(h)=b\cosh kh+d\sinh kh .
\end{equation*}
With $\Phi'(z)=kdz\cosh kz+\big[kb z+d\big]\sinh kz$ this is

\begin{equation*}
d=b\Big[\frac1{kh}-\tanh kh\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $C_{h}=\cosh kh,\;S_{h}=\sinh kh$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)=2k\Big[\,kbh+d\Big]C_{h}+2k^{2}dhS_{h}\; .
\end{equation*}
The basal shear

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}
\end{equation*}
gives after eliminating $d$

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=\Delta\rho g\,e\Big[\frac{kh}{C_{h}}+\frac{C_{h}}{kh}\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $\alpha=kh$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
       +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}}=
\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that in the notation $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
          =\Bigg[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
                +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad \alpha=kh ,
\end{equation*}
the sign being that of the $\sin kx$ basal traction.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\qquad\alpha\ll1,
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)\sim2\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0,\qquad\alpha\gg1,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the long wave limit

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and $e_{s}= \rho^{-1}g^{-1}S_{0}\,\alpha\cosh\alpha\,
[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]^{-1}$ with the plate density $\rho$ in the
normal stress condition $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho ge\cos kx$.  Up to the
conventional $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor this is the usual viscous
screening

$e_{s}=S_{0}\Delta\rho^{-1}g^{-1}[kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh]^{-1}$,
often written as $\chi_{0}=kh\cosh kh[(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh]^{-1}$.
The $\sin kx$ traction is $180^{\circ}$ out of phase with the $\cos kx$
topography, $e_{s}<0$ for the sign convention used above, the magnitude
being the $\chi$ quoted.  With the sign absorbed this is the familiar
form $e_{s}=S_{0}\Delta\rho^{-1}g^{-1}kh\sinh kh/[ \cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}]$
up to the conventional $\cosh\leftrightarrow\sinh$ phase choice. ∎

3.text:
With the stream function
$\psi =F(z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=F'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-kF\cos kx$,
$\nabla^{4}\psi=0$ gives on $0\le z\le h$, $z=0$ top,

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=\big(a+bz\big)\cosh kz+\big(c+dz\big)\sinh kz ,
\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big(F'''-k^{2}F'\big),\qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]\sin kx,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-\Big[P+2\mu kF'\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
The steady material surface is $u_{z}(0)=0$, $u_{x}(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
F(0)=0,\qquad F'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
hence $a=0$, $b=-kc$,

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=c\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+dz\sinh kz ,\qquad 
F'=\sinh kz\big[d-k^{2}cz\big]+dkz\cosh kz .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
F'''-k^{2}F'=-2k^{2}\big[kc\cosh kz-d\sinh kz\big]
\end{equation*}
and with the sign $p=P\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=2\mu k\big[kc\cosh kz-d\sinh kz\big],\qquad
P(0)=2\mu k^{2}c .
\end{equation*}
The free surface normal stress is

$\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)\cos kx=\rho ge\cos kx$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
P(0)=-\rho ge,\qquad 
c=-\frac{\rho ge}{2\mu k^{2}},
\end{equation*}
up to the usual $180^{\circ}$ sign.  With the zero normal traction at
the base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff P(h)+2\mu kF'(h)=0
\end{equation*}
one gets

\begin{equation*}
d=c\,\frac{kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh}{h\cosh kh},\qquad T=kh,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
F''+k^{2}F=2k\cosh kz\big[d-k^{2}cz\big]+2dk^{2}z\sinh kz,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{align}
\sigma_{xz}(h)&=\mu\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]_{h}\sin kx   \\
&=2\mu k\Big[d\big(\cosh T+ T\sinh T\big)-k^{2}ch\cosh T\Big]\sin kx .
\end{align}
Eliminating $d$,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=-\,\frac{2\mu kc}{h\cosh T}
\Big[T^{2}+\cosh^{2}T\Big]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
With $c=-\rho ge/2\mu k^{2}$

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \frac{\rho ge}{k}\,
\frac{T^{2}+\cosh^{2}T}{h\cosh T}
     =\rho ge\,\frac{T^{2}+\cosh^{2}T}{T\cosh T},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the steady state $\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{T\cosh T}{T^{2}+\cosh^{2}T},\qquad T=kh ,
\end{equation*}
the $-$ sign being the $180^{\circ}$ phase between the $\sin kx$ basal
shear and the $\cos kx$ topography.  In modulus

\begin{equation*}
|e_{s}|=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\chi_{\rho}(T),\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(T)=\frac{T\cosh T}{T^{2}+\cosh^{2}T}
        =\Big[\frac{T}{\cosh T}+\frac{\cosh T}{T}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that with the isostatic restoring stress $\sigma_{zz}(0)=
\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the conventionally quoted transfer function is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(T)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(T)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{T\cosh T}{T^{2}+\cosh^{2}T},
\qquad T=kh,
\end{equation*}
and for the usual geophysical identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(T)=\frac{T\cosh T}{T^{2}+\cosh^{2}T}
      =\Big[\frac{T}{\cosh T}+\frac{\cosh T}{T}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad T=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}=S_{0}\Delta\rho^{-1}g^{-1}
\big[T/\cosh T+\cosh T/T\big]^{-1}$.
In particular

\[
\chi(T)=T+O(T^{3}),\qquad T\ll1,\qquad 
\chi(T)\sim2T\,e^{-T}\to0,\qquad T\gg1,
\]

so that for long waves

\[
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}\,S_{0},\qquad
\chi\simeq kh ,
\]

and short wave basal tractions are exponentially screened,
$\chi\sim kh\,e^{-kh}$.  Up to the conventional $\sin\leftrightarrow\cos$
phase this is often written in modulus form

\[
|e_{s}|=\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\,\sinh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}},\qquad
\chi_{m}(kh)=\frac{kh\sinh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}},
\]

the $\sinh\leftrightarrow\cosh$ interchange being the $180^{\circ}$
phase between the imposed $\sin kx$ shear and the $\cos kx$ dynamic
topography.  The viscosity drops out of the steady response, as
required. ∎

8.text:
With the harmonic stream function

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx ,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$\nabla^{4}\psi=0$ is $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$ and with $z=0$ the free
top and $z=h$ the base

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(a+bz)\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz ,
\qquad 
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big], 
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= -\big[P+2\mu k\Phi'\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state $\dot e=0$ we may put $u_{z}(0)=0$,
$u_{x}(0)=0$ and hence

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that $a=0$, $b=-kc$ and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=c\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+dz\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=2\mu k\big[kc\cosh kz-d\sinh kz\big],\qquad 
P(0)=2\mu k^{2}c .
\end{equation*}
The free surface normal stress

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=\rho g e\cos kx
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
P(0)=- \rho g e,\qquad 
c=-\frac{\rho g\,e}{2\mu k^{2}},\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=\frac{k\rho g}{\mu}e  .
\end{equation*}
Zero normal traction at the base,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff P(h)+2\mu k\Phi'(h)=0,
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
c\cosh kh-c\big(1+kh\big)\sinh kh+dh\cosh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
d=c\,\frac{kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh}{h\cosh kh}\; .
\end{equation*}
Now

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi
   =2k\Big[d\cosh kz+d kz\sinh kz-k^{2}cz\cosh kz\Big]
\end{equation*}
and with the $d$ above

\begin{equation*}
\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
   =-\frac{2kc}{h\cosh kh}\Big[(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Hence the basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}\sin kx=S_{0}\sin kx
\end{equation*}
is

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= -\frac{2\mu kc}{h\cosh kh}\Big[(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big]
     =\frac{\rho g\,e}{k}\,
       \frac{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{h\cosh kh}\; .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state $\dot e=0$, $e=e_{s}$, and with $\alpha=kh$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
       \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
       \Bigg[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}+
             \frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1}\!,
\qquad\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
the sign being $e_{s}\propto-\sin kx$ for the $\sin kx$ basal shear.
The viscosity drops out.  Writing the isostatic restoring stress at the
free surface as $\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the transfer function is
conventionally written in the density contrast form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
      =\Bigg[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
            +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that with the plate density $\rho$ kept explicitly in the normal
stress condition

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
       \chi_{\rho}(\alpha)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}
       \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}\; .
\end{equation*}
For the usual geophysical identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this
is

\[
e_{s}= \chi\,\dfrac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\,\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigg[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Bigg]^{-1},
\]

and $e_{s}<0$ for the $\sin kx$ shear convention, i.e. in modulus

\[
|e_{s}|=\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\sinh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}\;,
\]

the conventional $\sinh$–numerator form which is the same transfer
function up to the $\sin\!\leftrightarrow\!\cos$ phase.  In particular

\[
\chi(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\qquad\alpha\ll1,
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)\sim \alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0,\qquad\alpha\gg1,
\]

so that for long waves

\[
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi\simeq kh,
\]

and the basal harmonic shear is exponentially screened for
$kh\gg1$.  With the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor restored this is the
viscosity-independent Stokes dynamic topography generated by the
harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$.  The $\sinh$–numerator form quoted
in the literature is the same expression up to the conventional sign
and $\sin\!\leftrightarrow\!\cos$ phase. ∎

4.text:
In the linear Stokes limit write with $z=0$ at the free surface and $z=h$
at the base

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=F(z)\sin kx\,,\qquad 
u_{x}=F'\sin kx\,,\qquad u_{z}=-kF\cos kx\,,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\big(D^{2}-k^{2}\big)^{2}F=0 ,\qquad 
F(z)=(a+bz)\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
and with $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big(F'''-k^{2}F'\big),\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big(F''+k^{2}F\big)\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-\Big[P+2\mu kF'\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state $\dot e=0$ the free surface is material,
$u_{x}(0)=0$, $u_{z}(0)=0$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
F(0)=0,\qquad F'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that $a=0$, $b+kc=0$ and, up to a scale,

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=c\Big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\Big]+dz\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
With the above

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=2\mu k\Big[kc\cosh kz-d\sinh kz\Big] ,
\qquad 
P(0)=2\mu k^{2}c .
\end{equation*}
The free surface normal stress

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=\rho g\,e\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
c=-\frac{\rho g\,e}{2\mu k^{2}},\qquad 
P(0)=-\rho g\,e .
\end{equation*}
The zero normal traction at the base,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff P(h)+2\mu kF'(h)=0,
\end{equation*}
is

\begin{equation*}
c\big[\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh\big]+dh\cosh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
d=c\,\frac{kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh}{h\cosh kh} .
\end{equation*}
Now

\begin{equation*}
F''+k^{2}F=2k\Big[-k^{2}cz\cosh kz+d\cosh kz
+dkz\sinh kz\Big],
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]_{h}
=-\,\frac{2kc}{h\cosh kh}\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]_{h}
=-\,\frac{2\mu kc}{h\cosh kh}\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big]
=S_{0} .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $c$ with $c=-\rho ge/2\mu k^{2}$,

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\rho g\,e}{k}\,
\frac{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{h\cosh kh}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the $t\to\infty$ steady state

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
       \Bigg[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad \alpha\equiv kh .
\end{equation*}
The viscosity cancels.  With the usual sign convention
$e_{s}>0$ for the $\cos kx$ normal stress the basal $\sin kx$ shear is
$180^{\circ}$ out of phase with the topography.  In modulus

\begin{equation*}
|e_{s}|=\chi_{\rho}(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}
       +\cosh^{2}\alpha}
     =\Big[\alpha/\cosh\alpha+\cosh\alpha/\alpha\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
$\alpha=kh$, so that

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\rho}\simeq\alpha,\qquad\alpha\ll1,\qquad
\chi_{\rho}\sim\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0,\qquad\alpha\gg1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\rho g$ long–wave limit and
exponential viscous screening for $kh\gg1$.

Writing the isostatic restoring stress at the free surface as
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the conventional
dynamic–topography form is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
          =\frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}
            \Big[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
                 +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
and with the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ in the
restoring-stress balance

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
      \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
           =\Big[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
                  +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
$\alpha=kh$, i.e. in magnitude

\[
e_{s}\approx\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
\Big[\,kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh\,\Big]^{-1},
\]

$ e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for $kh\ll1$ and
$e_{s}\propto kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  Up to the conventional
$\sin\!\leftrightarrow\!\cos$ phase the transfer function is often quoted
as

\[
|e_{s}|=\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\sinh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\]

the $\sinh$-numerator form being the same expression with the opposite
sign convention for the $180^{\circ}$ phase between the $\sin kx$ basal
shear and the $\cos kx$ topography.  With the density $\rho$ kept in
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$ this is $e_{s}=(\rho/\Delta\rho)
\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the viscosity-independent steady
Stokes sheet transfer function.  The $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor is unity
for the usual $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ isostatic convention. ∎

11.text:
In the harmonic mode put

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=F(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=F'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-kF\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}F=0,\qquad 
F(z)=(A+Bz)\cosh kz+(C+Dz)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
With $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[F'''-k^{2}F'\Big],\qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]\sin kx ,
\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=-\big[P+2\mu kF'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and $u_{x}(0)=0$, $u_{z}(0)=0$ in the steady state,
\begin{equation*}
F(0)=0,\qquad F'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with the origin at the free surface
\begin{equation*}
A=0,\qquad B=-kC,
\qquad 
F(z)=C\Big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\Big]+Dz\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=2\mu k^{2}C\cosh kz-2\mu kD\sinh kz,
\qquad 
P(0)=2\mu k^{2}C .
\end{equation*}
The free surface normal stress

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-\,P(0)=\rho g\,e\cos kx
\end{equation*}
gives
\begin{equation*}
C=-\frac{\rho g\,e}{2\mu k^{2}},\qquad 
P(0)=-\rho g e .
\end{equation*}
Zero normal traction at the base,
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$,
\begin{equation*}
P(h)+2\mu kF'(h)=0\iff
C\bigl[\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh\bigr]+Dh\cosh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
D=C\,\frac{kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh}{h\cosh kh}\;.
\end{equation*}
Now

\begin{equation*}
F''+k^{2}F=2k\Big[-k^{2}Cz\cosh kz
+D\cosh kz+Dkz\sinh kz\Big],
\end{equation*}
so that with $\alpha=kh$

\begin{equation*}
\Big[F''+k^{2}F\Big]_{h}
 =-\frac{2kC}{h\cosh\alpha}\Big[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha\Big] .
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\Big[F''+k^{2}F\Big]_{h}=S_{0}\sin kx
\end{equation*}
is therefore

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= -\,\frac{2\mu kC}{h\cosh\alpha}
\Big[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha\Big],
\end{equation*}
and with $C=-\rho g e/2\mu k^{2}$

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \frac{\rho g\,e}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
\Big[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Hence in the steady state $\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the conventional $180^{\circ}$ phase

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\Bigg[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
     +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}\;,
\end{equation*}
the sign being $e_{s}\propto-\sin kx$ for the $\sin kx$ basal traction.
Writing the restoring stress at the free surface as
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx$ the viscosity-independent
dynamic–topography transfer function is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
          =\Bigg[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
                +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
which for the usual geophysical identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$
is

\[
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
       =\Big[\alpha/\cosh\alpha+\cosh\alpha/\alpha\Big]^{-1},
\qquad\alpha=kh,
\]

so that

\[
\chi(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\qquad\alpha\ll1,\qquad
\chi(\alpha)\sim2\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0,\qquad\alpha\gg1,
\]

i.e. $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for $kh\ll1$ and exponential
Stokes screening for $kh\gg1$.
With the sign absorbed in the definition of $e_{s}$ the magnitude is
conventionally quoted as

\[
|e_{s}|=\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{\alpha\sinh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad 
\chi_{m}(\alpha)=\frac{kh\sinh kh}
{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}},
\]

which is the $\alpha\cosh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$ transfer
function up to the $180^{\circ}$ $\sin kx\!\leftrightarrow\!\cos kx$
phase.  The $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor is the usual change from the plate
density used in $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$ to the isostatic
buoyancy contrast $\Delta\rho$.  This is the viscosity-independent steady
Stokes sheet response to a harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$ with
$u_{x}(0)=0$, $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$. ∎

public_text.3:
With the stream function
$\psi =F(z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=F'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-kF\cos kx$,
$\nabla^{4}\psi=0$ gives on $0\le z\le h$, $z=0$ top,

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=\big(a+bz\big)\cosh kz+\big(c+dz\big)\sinh kz ,
\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big(F'''-k^{2}F'\big),\qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]\sin kx,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-\Big[P+2\mu kF'\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
The steady material surface is $u_{z}(0)=0$, $u_{x}(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
F(0)=0,\qquad F'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
hence $a=0$, $b=-kc$,

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=c\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+dz\sinh kz ,\qquad 
F'=\sinh kz\big[d-k^{2}cz\big]+dkz\cosh kz .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
F'''-k^{2}F'=-2k^{2}\big[kc\cosh kz-d\sinh kz\big]
\end{equation*}
and with the sign $p=P\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=2\mu k\big[kc\cosh kz-d\sinh kz\big],\qquad
P(0)=2\mu k^{2}c .
\end{equation*}
The free surface normal stress is

$\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)\cos kx=\rho ge\cos kx$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
P(0)=-\rho ge,\qquad 
c=-\frac{\rho ge}{2\mu k^{2}},
\end{equation*}
up to the usual $180^{\circ}$ sign.  With the zero normal traction at
the base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff P(h)+2\mu kF'(h)=0
\end{equation*}
one gets

\begin{equation*}
d=c\,\frac{kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh}{h\cosh kh},\qquad T=kh,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
F''+k^{2}F=2k\cosh kz\big[d-k^{2}cz\big]+2dk^{2}z\sinh kz,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{align}
\sigma_{xz}(h)&=\mu\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]_{h}\sin kx   \\
&=2\mu k\Big[d\big(\cosh T+ T\sinh T\big)-k^{2}ch\cosh T\Big]\sin kx .
\end{align}
Eliminating $d$,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=-\,\frac{2\mu kc}{h\cosh T}
\Big[T^{2}+\cosh^{2}T\Big]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
With $c=-\rho ge/2\mu k^{2}$

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \frac{\rho ge}{k}\,
\frac{T^{2}+\cosh^{2}T}{h\cosh T}
     =\rho ge\,\frac{T^{2}+\cosh^{2}T}{T\cosh T},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the steady state $\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{T\cosh T}{T^{2}+\cosh^{2}T},\qquad T=kh ,
\end{equation*}
the $-$ sign being the $180^{\circ}$ phase between the $\sin kx$ basal
shear and the $\cos kx$ topography.  In modulus

\begin{equation*}
|e_{s}|=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\chi_{\rho}(T),\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(T)=\frac{T\cosh T}{T^{2}+\cosh^{2}T}
        =\Big[\frac{T}{\cosh T}+\frac{\cosh T}{T}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that with the isostatic restoring stress $\sigma_{zz}(0)=
\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the conventionally quoted transfer function is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(T)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(T)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{T\cosh T}{T^{2}+\cosh^{2}T},
\qquad T=kh,
\end{equation*}
and for the usual geophysical identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(T)=\frac{T\cosh T}{T^{2}+\cosh^{2}T}
      =\Big[\frac{T}{\cosh T}+\frac{\cosh T}{T}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad T=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}=S_{0}\Delta\rho^{-1}g^{-1}
\big[T/\cosh T+\cosh T/T\big]^{-1}$.
In particular

\[
\chi(T)=T+O(T^{3}),\qquad T\ll1,\qquad 
\chi(T)\sim2T\,e^{-T}\to0,\qquad T\gg1,
\]

so that for long waves

\[
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}\,S_{0},\qquad
\chi\simeq kh ,
\]

and short wave basal tractions are exponentially screened,
$\chi\sim kh\,e^{-kh}$.  Up to the conventional $\sin\leftrightarrow\cos$
phase this is often written in modulus form

\[
|e_{s}|=\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\,\sinh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}},\qquad
\chi_{m}(kh)=\frac{kh\sinh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}},
\]

the $\sinh\leftrightarrow\cosh$ interchange being the $180^{\circ}$
phase between the imposed $\sin kx$ shear and the $\cos kx$ dynamic
topography.  The viscosity drops out of the steady response, as
required. ∎

public_text.8:
With the harmonic stream function

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx ,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$\nabla^{4}\psi=0$ is $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$ and with $z=0$ the free
top and $z=h$ the base

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(a+bz)\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz ,
\qquad 
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big], 
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= -\big[P+2\mu k\Phi'\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state $\dot e=0$ we may put $u_{z}(0)=0$,
$u_{x}(0)=0$ and hence

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that $a=0$, $b=-kc$ and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=c\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+dz\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=2\mu k\big[kc\cosh kz-d\sinh kz\big],\qquad 
P(0)=2\mu k^{2}c .
\end{equation*}
The free surface normal stress

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=\rho g e\cos kx
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
P(0)=- \rho g e,\qquad 
c=-\frac{\rho g\,e}{2\mu k^{2}},\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=\frac{k\rho g}{\mu}e  .
\end{equation*}
Zero normal traction at the base,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff P(h)+2\mu k\Phi'(h)=0,
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
c\cosh kh-c\big(1+kh\big)\sinh kh+dh\cosh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
d=c\,\frac{kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh}{h\cosh kh}\; .
\end{equation*}
Now

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi
   =2k\Big[d\cosh kz+d kz\sinh kz-k^{2}cz\cosh kz\Big]
\end{equation*}
and with the $d$ above

\begin{equation*}
\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
   =-\frac{2kc}{h\cosh kh}\Big[(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Hence the basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}\sin kx=S_{0}\sin kx
\end{equation*}
is

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= -\frac{2\mu kc}{h\cosh kh}\Big[(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big]
     =\frac{\rho g\,e}{k}\,
       \frac{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{h\cosh kh}\; .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state $\dot e=0$, $e=e_{s}$, and with $\alpha=kh$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
       \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
       \Bigg[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}+
             \frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1}\!,
\qquad\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
the sign being $e_{s}\propto-\sin kx$ for the $\sin kx$ basal shear.
The viscosity drops out.  Writing the isostatic restoring stress at the
free surface as $\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the transfer function is
conventionally written in the density contrast form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
      =\Bigg[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
            +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that with the plate density $\rho$ kept explicitly in the normal
stress condition

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
       \chi_{\rho}(\alpha)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}
       \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}\; .
\end{equation*}
For the usual geophysical identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this
is

\[
e_{s}= \chi\,\dfrac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\,\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigg[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Bigg]^{-1},
\]

and $e_{s}<0$ for the $\sin kx$ shear convention, i.e. in modulus

\[
|e_{s}|=\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\sinh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}\;,
\]

the conventional $\sinh$–numerator form which is the same transfer
function up to the $\sin\!\leftrightarrow\!\cos$ phase.  In particular

\[
\chi(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\qquad\alpha\ll1,
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)\sim \alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0,\qquad\alpha\gg1,
\]

so that for long waves

\[
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi\simeq kh,
\]

and the basal harmonic shear is exponentially screened for
$kh\gg1$.  With the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor restored this is the
viscosity-independent Stokes dynamic topography generated by the
harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$.  The $\sinh$–numerator form quoted
in the literature is the same expression up to the conventional sign
and $\sin\!\leftrightarrow\!\cos$ phase. ∎

public_text.4:
In the linear Stokes limit write with $z=0$ at the free surface and $z=h$
at the base

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=F(z)\sin kx\,,\qquad 
u_{x}=F'\sin kx\,,\qquad u_{z}=-kF\cos kx\,,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\big(D^{2}-k^{2}\big)^{2}F=0 ,\qquad 
F(z)=(a+bz)\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
and with $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big(F'''-k^{2}F'\big),\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big(F''+k^{2}F\big)\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-\Big[P+2\mu kF'\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state $\dot e=0$ the free surface is material,
$u_{x}(0)=0$, $u_{z}(0)=0$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
F(0)=0,\qquad F'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that $a=0$, $b+kc=0$ and, up to a scale,

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=c\Big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\Big]+dz\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
With the above

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=2\mu k\Big[kc\cosh kz-d\sinh kz\Big] ,
\qquad 
P(0)=2\mu k^{2}c .
\end{equation*}
The free surface normal stress

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=\rho g\,e\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
c=-\frac{\rho g\,e}{2\mu k^{2}},\qquad 
P(0)=-\rho g\,e .
\end{equation*}
The zero normal traction at the base,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff P(h)+2\mu kF'(h)=0,
\end{equation*}
is

\begin{equation*}
c\big[\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh\big]+dh\cosh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
d=c\,\frac{kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh}{h\cosh kh} .
\end{equation*}
Now

\begin{equation*}
F''+k^{2}F=2k\Big[-k^{2}cz\cosh kz+d\cosh kz
+dkz\sinh kz\Big],
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]_{h}
=-\,\frac{2kc}{h\cosh kh}\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]_{h}
=-\,\frac{2\mu kc}{h\cosh kh}\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big]
=S_{0} .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $c$ with $c=-\rho ge/2\mu k^{2}$,

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\rho g\,e}{k}\,
\frac{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{h\cosh kh}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the $t\to\infty$ steady state

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
       \Bigg[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad \alpha\equiv kh .
\end{equation*}
The viscosity cancels.  With the usual sign convention
$e_{s}>0$ for the $\cos kx$ normal stress the basal $\sin kx$ shear is
$180^{\circ}$ out of phase with the topography.  In modulus

\begin{equation*}
|e_{s}|=\chi_{\rho}(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}
       +\cosh^{2}\alpha}
     =\Big[\alpha/\cosh\alpha+\cosh\alpha/\alpha\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
$\alpha=kh$, so that

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\rho}\simeq\alpha,\qquad\alpha\ll1,\qquad
\chi_{\rho}\sim\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0,\qquad\alpha\gg1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\rho g$ long–wave limit and
exponential viscous screening for $kh\gg1$.

Writing the isostatic restoring stress at the free surface as
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the conventional
dynamic–topography form is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
          =\frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}
            \Big[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
                 +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
and with the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ in the
restoring-stress balance

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
      \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
           =\Big[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
                  +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
$\alpha=kh$, i.e. in magnitude

\[
e_{s}\approx\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
\Big[\,kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh\,\Big]^{-1},
\]

$ e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for $kh\ll1$ and
$e_{s}\propto kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  Up to the conventional
$\sin\!\leftrightarrow\!\cos$ phase the transfer function is often quoted
as

\[
|e_{s}|=\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\sinh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\]

the $\sinh$-numerator form being the same expression with the opposite
sign convention for the $180^{\circ}$ phase between the $\sin kx$ basal
shear and the $\cos kx$ topography.  With the density $\rho$ kept in
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$ this is $e_{s}=(\rho/\Delta\rho)
\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the viscosity-independent steady
Stokes sheet transfer function.  The $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor is unity
for the usual $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ isostatic convention. ∎

public_text.11:
In the harmonic mode put

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=F(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=F'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-kF\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}F=0,\qquad 
F(z)=(A+Bz)\cosh kz+(C+Dz)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
With $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[F'''-k^{2}F'\Big],\qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]\sin kx ,
\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=-\big[P+2\mu kF'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and $u_{x}(0)=0$, $u_{z}(0)=0$ in the steady state,
\begin{equation*}
F(0)=0,\qquad F'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with the origin at the free surface
\begin{equation*}
A=0,\qquad B=-kC,
\qquad 
F(z)=C\Big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\Big]+Dz\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=2\mu k^{2}C\cosh kz-2\mu kD\sinh kz,
\qquad 
P(0)=2\mu k^{2}C .
\end{equation*}
The free surface normal stress

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-\,P(0)=\rho g\,e\cos kx
\end{equation*}
gives
\begin{equation*}
C=-\frac{\rho g\,e}{2\mu k^{2}},\qquad 
P(0)=-\rho g e .
\end{equation*}
Zero normal traction at the base,
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$,
\begin{equation*}
P(h)+2\mu kF'(h)=0\iff
C\bigl[\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh\bigr]+Dh\cosh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
D=C\,\frac{kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh}{h\cosh kh}\;.
\end{equation*}
Now

\begin{equation*}
F''+k^{2}F=2k\Big[-k^{2}Cz\cosh kz
+D\cosh kz+Dkz\sinh kz\Big],
\end{equation*}
so that with $\alpha=kh$

\begin{equation*}
\Big[F''+k^{2}F\Big]_{h}
 =-\frac{2kC}{h\cosh\alpha}\Big[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha\Big] .
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\Big[F''+k^{2}F\Big]_{h}=S_{0}\sin kx
\end{equation*}
is therefore

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= -\,\frac{2\mu kC}{h\cosh\alpha}
\Big[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha\Big],
\end{equation*}
and with $C=-\rho g e/2\mu k^{2}$

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \frac{\rho g\,e}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
\Big[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Hence in the steady state $\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the conventional $180^{\circ}$ phase

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\Bigg[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
     +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}\;,
\end{equation*}
the sign being $e_{s}\propto-\sin kx$ for the $\sin kx$ basal traction.
Writing the restoring stress at the free surface as
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx$ the viscosity-independent
dynamic–topography transfer function is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
          =\Bigg[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
                +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
which for the usual geophysical identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$
is

\[
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
       =\Big[\alpha/\cosh\alpha+\cosh\alpha/\alpha\Big]^{-1},
\qquad\alpha=kh,
\]

so that

\[
\chi(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\qquad\alpha\ll1,\qquad
\chi(\alpha)\sim2\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0,\qquad\alpha\gg1,
\]

i.e. $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for $kh\ll1$ and exponential
Stokes screening for $kh\gg1$.
With the sign absorbed in the definition of $e_{s}$ the magnitude is
conventionally quoted as

\[
|e_{s}|=\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{\alpha\sinh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad 
\chi_{m}(\alpha)=\frac{kh\sinh kh}
{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}},
\]

which is the $\alpha\cosh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$ transfer
function up to the $180^{\circ}$ $\sin kx\!\leftrightarrow\!\cos kx$
phase.  The $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor is the usual change from the plate
density used in $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$ to the isostatic
buoyancy contrast $\Delta\rho$.  This is the viscosity-independent steady
Stokes sheet response to a harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$ with
$u_{x}(0)=0$, $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$. ∎

6.text:
With the stream function $\psi (x,z)=F(z)\sin kx$ and
$p(x,z)=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=F'\sin kx ,\qquad u_{z}=-kF\cos kx ,\qquad
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[F'''-k^{2}F'\Big] ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\Big[F''+k^{2}F\Big]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=-\Big[P+2\mu kF'\Big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}F=0$ and with $z=0$ the free surface and $z=h$ the
base

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=(a+bz)\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
In the steady material surface $\dot e=0$
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad u_{z}(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. for the steady mode

\begin{equation*}
F(0)=0,\qquad F'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that $a=0$, $b=-kc$ and

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=c\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+dz\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P(z)=2\mu k\big[kc\cosh kz-d\sinh kz\big],\qquad
P(0)=2\mu k^{2}c .
\end{equation*}
The free surface normal stress

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=\rho g e\cos kx
\end{equation*}
gives
\begin{equation*}
P(0)=-\rho ge,\qquad c=-\frac{\rho g e}{2\mu k^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
The stress free base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff P(h)+2\mu kF'(h)=0
\end{equation*}
is
\begin{equation*}
c\big[\cosh\alpha-\alpha\sinh\alpha\big]+dh\cosh\alpha=0,
\qquad\alpha\equiv kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
d=c\,\frac{\alpha\sinh\alpha-\cosh\alpha}{h\cosh\alpha}\; .
\end{equation*}
Now
\begin{equation*}
F''+k^{2}F=2k\Big[d\cosh kz-k^{2}cz\cosh kz+dkz\sinh kz\Big]
\end{equation*}
so that at $z=h$
\begin{equation*}
\Big[F''+k^{2}F\Big]_{h}
=-\frac{2kc}{h\cosh\alpha}\Big[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha\Big],
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\Big[F''+k^{2}F\Big]_{h}
=-\frac{2\mu kc}{h\cosh\alpha}\Big[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha\Big]
=S_{0}\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $c$ with $c=-\rho ge/2\mu k^{2}$,

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \rho g\,e\,
\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha},
\qquad\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the steady $t\to\infty$ limit $\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
      =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
\Bigg[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
      +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
the $-$ sign being the $180^{o}$ phase between the imposed
$\sin kx$ basal shear and the $\cos kx$ topography.  The viscosity
cancels.  Writing the isostatic restoring stress at the free surface as
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the conventionally quoted
dynamic-topography transfer function is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
      =\Bigg[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
            +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
with
\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad\alpha=kh .
\end{equation*}
For the usual geophysical identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ the
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor is unity and

\[
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\chi(kh),\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\,\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Big[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Big]^{-1},
\]

so that
\[
\chi\simeq kh+O\big((kh)^{3}\big),\;kh\ll1,\qquad
\chi\sim kh\,e^{-kh}\to0,\;kh\gg1,
\]
i.e. $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for long waves and exponential
Stokes screening for $kh\gg1$.  In modulus form the $180^{o}$
$\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ phase is conventionally absorbed and the
transfer function is quoted as

\[
|e_{s}|=\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\chi_{m}(kh),\qquad 
\chi_{m}(kh)=\frac{kh\,\sinh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\]

the $\sinh\leftrightarrow\cosh$ interchange being the usual sign/phase
convention for the $S_{0}\sin kx$ basal traction.  With the $\rho$ kept
in $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho ge\cos kx$ this is $e_{s}= (\rho/\Delta\rho)
\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the viscosity independent steady Stokes
sheet screening factor.  The $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ form is the
$\alpha\cosh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$ result normally quoted,
$|e_{s}|=S_{0}/\Delta\rho g\;\alpha\sinh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$
in magnitude, up to the conventional $180^{o}$ phase. ∎

11.text:
For the harmonic mode put $z=0$ at the free top and $z=h$ at the base

\begin{equation*}
\psi=F(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=F'\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{z}=-kF\cos kx,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla^{4}\psi=0$,

\begin{equation*}
(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}F=0,\qquad 
F(z)=(a+bz)\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[F'''-k^{2}F'\Big],\qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\Big[F''+k^{2}F\Big]\sin kx,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-\Big[P+2\mu kF'\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state $e_{t}=0$ and the only independent kinematic
condition at the free surface is $u_{x}(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
F'(0)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
With $F'(0)=0$ we may put

\begin{equation*}
a=0,\qquad b+ k c=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with $a_{1}=b$

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=a_{1}\Big[z\cosh kz-\frac{\sinh kz}{k}\Big]+dz\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-2\mu k\Big[a_{1}\cosh kz+d\sinh kz\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=2\mu k a_{1}=\rho g e ,
\qquad 
a_{1}=\frac{\rho g}{2\mu k}e ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff 
a_{1}\cosh kh-\frac{a_{1}}{k}k\sinh kh+dh\cosh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
d=a_{1}\frac{1}{kh}-\tanh kh ,
\qquad d=\frac{a_{1}}{k}\frac{kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh}{h\cosh kh}.
\end{equation*}
Now

\begin{equation*}
F''+k^{2}F=2k\Big[(k a_{1}z+d)\cosh kz+kdz\sinh kz\Big]
\end{equation*}
and with the $d$ above

\begin{equation*}
\Big[F''+k^{2}F\Big]_{h}
   =\frac{2k a_{1}}{h\cosh kh}
     \Big[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}\Big]\,
     \frac{\sinh kh}{\cosh kh} .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=\mu\Big[F''+k^{2}F\Big]_{h}
     =\frac{\rho g e}{kh}
       \frac{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}{\sinh kh} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the $t\to\infty$ material steady state $e_{t}=0$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
       \frac{kh\,\sinh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}},\qquad 
       \alpha\equiv kh ,
\end{equation*}
the $-$ sign being the $180^{o}$ phase between the imposed
$S_{0}\sin kx$ basal shear and the $\cos kx$ topography.  The viscosity
cancels.  Putting the isostatic restoring stress at the free surface in
the conventional form $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the
viscosity independent Stokes sheet transfer function can be written as

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
       =\Big[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
            +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that with the $\rho$ used in $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho ge\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\;
       \chi_{\rho}(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}
            \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
                 {\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}\;,
\end{equation*}
and for the usual geophysical identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
           {\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
         =\Big[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
               +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad \alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
so that with the sign absorbed

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}\,S_{0},\qquad kh\ll1,
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)\sim\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0,\qquad kh\gg1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous screening of a harmonic basal traction.  In
magnitude the $180^{\circ}$ $\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ phase is
conventionally absorbed and the transfer function is quoted as

\begin{equation*}
|e_{s}|=\chi_{m}(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{m}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\sinh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},\qquad\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $ |e_{s}|=S_{0}/\Delta\rho g\;[\alpha/\cosh\alpha+\cosh\alpha/\alpha
]^{-1}$ up to the sign.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{m}(\alpha)=\alpha^{2}+O(\alpha^{4}),\;\alpha\ll1,\qquad
\chi_{m}(\alpha)\sim\alpha e^{-\alpha},\;\alpha\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that $e_{s}\propto(kh)^{2}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for a very long wave
and the short wavelength basal traction is exponentially screened.
With the sign convention $e_{s}\propto-\sin kx$ the $\sinh$ numerator is
the $\cosh$ numerator $\alpha\cosh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$
up to the usual $180^{o}$ phase, and the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor is
unity for the usual $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ isostatic convention.  This
is the viscosity independent steady dynamic topography generated by the
harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$ with $u_{x}(0)=0$, 
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ and $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$. ∎

10.text:
In the harmonic mode write with $z=0$ at the free top and $z=h$ at the
base

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=F(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=F'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-kF\cos kx,
\qquad p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\nabla^{4}\psi =0,\qquad 
F(z)=(a+bz)\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[F'''-k^{2}F'\Big],\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]\sin kx,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-\Big[P+2\mu kF'\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state $\dot e=u_{z}(0)=0$ and the only independent
kinematic condition at the free surface is $u_{x}(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
F(0)=0,\qquad F'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with $a=0$, $b=-kc$

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=c\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+dz\sinh kz ,
\qquad 
P(z)=2\mu k\big[kc\cosh kz-d\sinh kz\big],
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
P(0)=2\mu k^{2}c=-\rho g e ,\qquad 
c=-\frac{\rho g e}{2\mu k^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
The zero normal traction at the base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff P(h)+2\mu kF'(h)=0
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
c\big[\cosh\alpha-\alpha\sinh\alpha\big]+dh\cosh\alpha=0,
\qquad\alpha\equiv kh,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
d=c\,\frac{\alpha\sinh\alpha-\cosh\alpha}{h\cosh\alpha}\; .
\end{equation*}
Now

\begin{equation*}
F''+k^{2}F=2k\Big[-k^{2}cz\cosh kz+d\cosh kz
+dkz\sinh kz\Big],
\end{equation*}
so that at $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]_{h}
=-\frac{2kc}{h\cosh\alpha}\Big[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha\Big],
\end{equation*}
and with $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \mu\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]_{h}
     =-\frac{2\mu kc}{h\cosh\alpha}
       \Big[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $c$ with $c=-\rho ge/2\mu k^{2}$,

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \rho g e\,
\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}\;,
\qquad\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the $t\to\infty$ limit $\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
       \Big[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}+\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
the $180^{\circ}$ $\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ phase being absorbed in
the sign of $e_{s}$.  The viscosity drops out of the steady amplitude.
Writing the isostatic restoring stress as 
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the conventional
dynamic-topography transfer function is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
      =\Big[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
           +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\chi_{\rho}(\alpha),\qquad \alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
so that for the usual geophysical identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
       =\Big[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
             +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}S_{0}\;\;kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi(\alpha)\sim\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$, the familiar
viscous Stokes screening.  In modulus form the $180^{\circ}$ phase
between the imposed $S_{0}\sin kx$ basal shear and the $\cos kx$
topography is often written as

\begin{equation*}
|e_{s}|=\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\chi_{m}(\alpha),\qquad 
\chi_{m}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\sinh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\chi_{m}= \alpha\sinh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$,
$\chi_{m}\simeq\alpha^{2}$ for $\alpha\ll1$ and
$\chi_{m}\sim\alpha e^{-\alpha}$ for $\alpha\gg1$, which is the
$\alpha\cosh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$ transfer function up to
the conventional $\sin\leftrightarrow\cos$ sign/phase.  With the
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ normalisation restored this is the viscosity
independent steady Stokes sheet response $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
to the harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$ with $u_{x}(0)=0$,
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ and $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho ge\cos kx$.  Up to the
usual $180^{\circ}$ sign this is the quoted
$e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ long-wave limit and exponential
screening for $kh\gg1$. ∎

8.text:
With $z=0$ at the free surface and $z=h$ at the base put

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
 \nabla^{4}\psi=0,\quad 
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big],
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=-\big[P+2\mu k\Phi'\big]\cos kx  .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state $\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad u_{z}(0)=0,
\qquad\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=c\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+dz\sinh kz,
\qquad 
P(z)=2\mu k^{2}c\cosh kz-2\mu kd\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
The free surface normal stress is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=\rho ge\cos kx,
\qquad P(0)=2\mu k^{2}c=-\rho ge,
\qquad 
c=-\frac{\rho ge}{2\mu k^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff P(h)+2\mu k\Phi'(h)=0
\iff\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
c\cosh\alpha-c\alpha\sinh\alpha+dh\cosh\alpha=0,
\qquad\alpha\equiv kh,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
d=c\,\frac{\alpha\sinh\alpha-\cosh\alpha}{h\cosh\alpha}.
\end{equation*}
Now

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi=2k\cosh kz\big[d-k^{2}cz\big]
+2dk^{2}z\sinh kz,
\end{equation*}
and at $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
=-\frac{2kc}{h\cosh\alpha}\Big[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha\Big].
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
=-\frac{2\mu kc}{h\cosh\alpha}
\Big[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha\Big]
=S_{0}\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
With $c=-\rho ge/2\mu k^{2}$

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \rho g e\,
\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha},
\qquad\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the $t\to\infty$ steady state, up to the conventional
$180^{\circ}$ $\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ sign,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
       \Big[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
            +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
which is viscosity independent.  Writing the restoring stress at the
free surface as $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the conventional
dynamic–topography transfer function is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
     =\Big[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
           +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
and with the usual geophysical identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}S_{0}\;\;kh\ll1,
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)\sim\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0\;\;kh\gg1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous screening of a harmonic basal traction.  Up to the
conventional $\sin\!\leftrightarrow\!\cos$ phase the modulus is often
quoted as

\begin{equation*}
|e_{s}|=\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\sinh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\qquad 
\chi_{m}(kh)=\frac{kh\sinh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}},
\end{equation*}
the $\sinh\leftrightarrow\cosh$ interchange being the $180^{\circ}$
phase between the $S_{0}\sin kx$ basal shear and the $\cos kx$
topography.  The viscosity cancels in the steady amplitude and
$\chi\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$, the Stokes screening of short
wavelength basal tractions.  This is the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ normalised
form of the $e_{s}=S_{0}\rho^{-1}g^{-1}\alpha\cosh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}
+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$ result. ∎

public_text.6:
With the stream function $\psi (x,z)=F(z)\sin kx$ and
$p(x,z)=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=F'\sin kx ,\qquad u_{z}=-kF\cos kx ,\qquad
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[F'''-k^{2}F'\Big] ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\Big[F''+k^{2}F\Big]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=-\Big[P+2\mu kF'\Big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}F=0$ and with $z=0$ the free surface and $z=h$ the
base

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=(a+bz)\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
In the steady material surface $\dot e=0$
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad u_{z}(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. for the steady mode

\begin{equation*}
F(0)=0,\qquad F'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that $a=0$, $b=-kc$ and

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=c\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+dz\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P(z)=2\mu k\big[kc\cosh kz-d\sinh kz\big],\qquad
P(0)=2\mu k^{2}c .
\end{equation*}
The free surface normal stress

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=\rho g e\cos kx
\end{equation*}
gives
\begin{equation*}
P(0)=-\rho ge,\qquad c=-\frac{\rho g e}{2\mu k^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
The stress free base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff P(h)+2\mu kF'(h)=0
\end{equation*}
is
\begin{equation*}
c\big[\cosh\alpha-\alpha\sinh\alpha\big]+dh\cosh\alpha=0,
\qquad\alpha\equiv kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
d=c\,\frac{\alpha\sinh\alpha-\cosh\alpha}{h\cosh\alpha}\; .
\end{equation*}
Now
\begin{equation*}
F''+k^{2}F=2k\Big[d\cosh kz-k^{2}cz\cosh kz+dkz\sinh kz\Big]
\end{equation*}
so that at $z=h$
\begin{equation*}
\Big[F''+k^{2}F\Big]_{h}
=-\frac{2kc}{h\cosh\alpha}\Big[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha\Big],
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\Big[F''+k^{2}F\Big]_{h}
=-\frac{2\mu kc}{h\cosh\alpha}\Big[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha\Big]
=S_{0}\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $c$ with $c=-\rho ge/2\mu k^{2}$,

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \rho g\,e\,
\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha},
\qquad\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the steady $t\to\infty$ limit $\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
      =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
\Bigg[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
      +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
the $-$ sign being the $180^{o}$ phase between the imposed
$\sin kx$ basal shear and the $\cos kx$ topography.  The viscosity
cancels.  Writing the isostatic restoring stress at the free surface as
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the conventionally quoted
dynamic-topography transfer function is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
      =\Bigg[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
            +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
with
\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad\alpha=kh .
\end{equation*}
For the usual geophysical identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ the
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor is unity and

\[
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\chi(kh),\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\,\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Big[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Big]^{-1},
\]

so that
\[
\chi\simeq kh+O\big((kh)^{3}\big),\;kh\ll1,\qquad
\chi\sim kh\,e^{-kh}\to0,\;kh\gg1,
\]
i.e. $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for long waves and exponential
Stokes screening for $kh\gg1$.  In modulus form the $180^{o}$
$\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ phase is conventionally absorbed and the
transfer function is quoted as

\[
|e_{s}|=\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\chi_{m}(kh),\qquad 
\chi_{m}(kh)=\frac{kh\,\sinh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\]

the $\sinh\leftrightarrow\cosh$ interchange being the usual sign/phase
convention for the $S_{0}\sin kx$ basal traction.  With the $\rho$ kept
in $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho ge\cos kx$ this is $e_{s}= (\rho/\Delta\rho)
\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the viscosity independent steady Stokes
sheet screening factor.  The $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ form is the
$\alpha\cosh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$ result normally quoted,
$|e_{s}|=S_{0}/\Delta\rho g\;\alpha\sinh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$
in magnitude, up to the conventional $180^{o}$ phase. ∎

public_text.11:
For the harmonic mode put $z=0$ at the free top and $z=h$ at the base

\begin{equation*}
\psi=F(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=F'\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{z}=-kF\cos kx,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla^{4}\psi=0$,

\begin{equation*}
(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}F=0,\qquad 
F(z)=(a+bz)\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[F'''-k^{2}F'\Big],\qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\Big[F''+k^{2}F\Big]\sin kx,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-\Big[P+2\mu kF'\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state $e_{t}=0$ and the only independent kinematic
condition at the free surface is $u_{x}(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
F'(0)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
With $F'(0)=0$ we may put

\begin{equation*}
a=0,\qquad b+ k c=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with $a_{1}=b$

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=a_{1}\Big[z\cosh kz-\frac{\sinh kz}{k}\Big]+dz\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-2\mu k\Big[a_{1}\cosh kz+d\sinh kz\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=2\mu k a_{1}=\rho g e ,
\qquad 
a_{1}=\frac{\rho g}{2\mu k}e ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff 
a_{1}\cosh kh-\frac{a_{1}}{k}k\sinh kh+dh\cosh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
d=a_{1}\frac{1}{kh}-\tanh kh ,
\qquad d=\frac{a_{1}}{k}\frac{kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh}{h\cosh kh}.
\end{equation*}
Now

\begin{equation*}
F''+k^{2}F=2k\Big[(k a_{1}z+d)\cosh kz+kdz\sinh kz\Big]
\end{equation*}
and with the $d$ above

\begin{equation*}
\Big[F''+k^{2}F\Big]_{h}
   =\frac{2k a_{1}}{h\cosh kh}
     \Big[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}\Big]\,
     \frac{\sinh kh}{\cosh kh} .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=\mu\Big[F''+k^{2}F\Big]_{h}
     =\frac{\rho g e}{kh}
       \frac{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}{\sinh kh} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the $t\to\infty$ material steady state $e_{t}=0$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
       \frac{kh\,\sinh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}},\qquad 
       \alpha\equiv kh ,
\end{equation*}
the $-$ sign being the $180^{o}$ phase between the imposed
$S_{0}\sin kx$ basal shear and the $\cos kx$ topography.  The viscosity
cancels.  Putting the isostatic restoring stress at the free surface in
the conventional form $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the
viscosity independent Stokes sheet transfer function can be written as

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
       =\Big[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
            +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that with the $\rho$ used in $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho ge\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\;
       \chi_{\rho}(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}
            \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
                 {\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}\;,
\end{equation*}
and for the usual geophysical identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
           {\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
         =\Big[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
               +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad \alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
so that with the sign absorbed

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}\,S_{0},\qquad kh\ll1,
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)\sim\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0,\qquad kh\gg1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous screening of a harmonic basal traction.  In
magnitude the $180^{\circ}$ $\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ phase is
conventionally absorbed and the transfer function is quoted as

\begin{equation*}
|e_{s}|=\chi_{m}(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{m}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\sinh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},\qquad\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $ |e_{s}|=S_{0}/\Delta\rho g\;[\alpha/\cosh\alpha+\cosh\alpha/\alpha
]^{-1}$ up to the sign.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{m}(\alpha)=\alpha^{2}+O(\alpha^{4}),\;\alpha\ll1,\qquad
\chi_{m}(\alpha)\sim\alpha e^{-\alpha},\;\alpha\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that $e_{s}\propto(kh)^{2}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for a very long wave
and the short wavelength basal traction is exponentially screened.
With the sign convention $e_{s}\propto-\sin kx$ the $\sinh$ numerator is
the $\cosh$ numerator $\alpha\cosh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$
up to the usual $180^{o}$ phase, and the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor is
unity for the usual $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ isostatic convention.  This
is the viscosity independent steady dynamic topography generated by the
harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$ with $u_{x}(0)=0$, 
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ and $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$. ∎

