public_text.3:
With $z=0$ the free top and $z=h$ the base put

\begin{equation*}
\psi =F(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=F'\sin kx ,\qquad u_{z}=-kF\cos kx ,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}F=0$ and with the usual stream function
normalisation

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[F'''-k^{2}F'\big],\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\,[F''+k^{2}F]\sin kx ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-\big[P+2\mu kF'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
F(z)=(a+bz)\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
On the material top

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\iff F'(0)=0,\qquad \dot e=u_{z}(0)=-kF(0)\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
so that in the $t\to\infty$ steady state $\dot e=0$,

\begin{equation*}
F(0)=0,\qquad F'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $a=0,\;b=-kc$ and

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=c\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+dz\sinh kz,\qquad
P(z)=2\mu k\big[kc\cosh kz-d\sinh kz\big],
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P(0)=2\mu k^{2}c .
\end{equation*}
The normal stress on the deflected top is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=\rho g e\cos kx,\qquad 
c=-\frac{\rho g e}{2\mu k^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
The stress free base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is $P(h)+2\mu kF'(h)=0$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c\big[\cosh\alpha-\alpha\sinh\alpha\big]+dh\cosh\alpha=0,
\qquad\alpha\equiv kh,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
d=c\,\frac{\alpha\sinh\alpha-\cosh\alpha}{h\cosh\alpha}\; .
\end{equation*}
Now

\begin{equation*}
F''+k^{2}F=2k\Big[kdz\sinh kz+\big(d-k^{2}cz\big)\cosh kz\Big],
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]_{h}
=-\,\frac{2kc}{h\cosh\alpha}\Big[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha\Big],
\end{equation*}
and the imposed basal shear $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$ gives

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=\mu\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]_{h}
     =-\frac{2\mu kc}{h\cosh\alpha}
       \Big[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha\Big].
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $c$,

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \rho g\,e\,
\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha},
\qquad\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the conventional $180^{\circ}\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$
sign

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
       \Big[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
            +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
and the viscosity cancels in the steady amplitude.  Writing the
isostatic restoring traction at the free surface in the conventional
form $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the viscosity independent
dynamic topography transfer function can be written as

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi_{\rho}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
      =\Big[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
            +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\chi_{\rho}(\alpha),\qquad\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
so that for the usual geophysical identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
          =\Big[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}S_{0},\qquad kh\ll1,
\qquad\chi(\alpha)\sim kh\,e^{-kh}\to0,\qquad kh\gg1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar Stokes screening of a harmonic basal traction.  In
modulus form the $180^{\circ}$ $\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ phase is
conventionally absorbed and the transfer function is quoted as

\begin{equation*}
\big|e_{s}\big|=
\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\chi_{m}(\alpha),\qquad 
\chi_{m}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\sinh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $|\chi_{m}|\simeq\alpha^{2}$ for $\alpha\ll1$ and 
$\chi_{m}\sim\alpha e^{-\alpha}$ for $\alpha\gg1$, which is the
$\alpha\cosh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$ result up to the
usual $\sin\leftrightarrow\cos$ sign/phase.  With the
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$ normalisation $\rho/\Delta\rho=1$ this is
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the screening factor
$\chi^{-1}= \alpha/\cosh\alpha+\cosh\alpha/\alpha$, the viscosity
independent steady Stokes sheet response to the harmonic basal shear
$S_{0}\sin kx$ with $u_{x}(0)=0$, $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ and
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho ge\cos kx$.  This is the
$\chi\simeq kh$ long wave limit and the exponential $e^{-kh}$ short wave
screening normally quoted in the literature. ∎

public_text.8:
With the harmonic mode $z=0$ at the top and $z=h$ at the base

\begin{equation*}
\psi=F(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=F'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-kF\cos kx,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[F'''-k^{2}F'\big],\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]\sin kx,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-\big[P+2\mu kF'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}F=0$,

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=(a+bz)\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
the four independent Stokes data for the instantaneous flow are

* $u_{x}(0)=0\iff F'(0)=0$,
* $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e(t)\cos kx$,
* $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$,
* $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$,

and the kinematic coupling of the deflected surface is
$u_{z}(0)=\dot e$, i.e. $-kF(0)=\dot e$.  In the steady material limit
$\dot e\to0$ we have $F(0)\to0$ as a consequence of the evolution,
not as an independent biharmonic condition.  The independent kinematic
condition at the free surface is therefore $F'(0)=0$ only.

Put $a=0$, $b+kc=0$, $a_{1}=b$,
\begin{equation*}
F(z)=a_{1}\!\left[z\cosh kz-\frac{\sinh kz}{k}\right]+dz\sinh kz,
\qquad 
P(z)=-2\mu k\big[a_{1}\cosh kz+d\sinh kz\big],
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=2\mu k a_{1}=\rho g e ,\qquad 
a_{1}=\frac{\rho g}{2\mu k}e .
\end{equation*}
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ gives $P(h)+2\mu kF'(h)=0$ i.e.

\begin{equation*}
a_{1}\cosh\alpha-\frac{a_{1}}{k}k\sinh\alpha+dh\cosh\alpha=0,
\qquad\alpha\equiv kh,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
d=a_{1}\frac{1}{kh}-\tanh kh
   =\frac{a_{1}}{k}\frac{kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh}{h\cosh kh}\; .
\end{equation*}
Then
\begin{equation*}
F''+k^{2}F=2k\Big[(ka_{1}z+d)\cosh kz+dkz\sinh kz\Big]
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]_{h}
 =\frac{2ka_{1}}{h\cosh\alpha}
   \Big[\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}\Big]\,
   \frac{\sinh\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence with $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}$,

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \mu\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]_{h}
     =\frac{\rho g e}{kh}
       \frac{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}{\sinh\alpha}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the $t\to\infty\;\dot e=0$ limit

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
       \frac{\alpha\sinh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
the minus sign being the $180^{o}$ $\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$
phase between the imposed basal shear and the topography.  The
viscosity cancels in the steady amplitude.  Writing the isostatic
restoring traction in the conventional form
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\sinh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}},\qquad
\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
so that for the usual geophysical identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{m}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\sinh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
      =\Big[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
            +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Big]^{-1}\!\!_{\sin},
\qquad 
e_{s}\propto \chi_{m}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g .
\end{equation*}
In modulus the $90^{\circ}$ phase $\sin kx\to\cos kx$ is conventionally
absorbed and the transfer function is quoted as

\begin{equation*}
|e_{s}|=\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
      =\Big[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
            +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential $180^{o}$ sign
$e_{s}=S_{0}/\Delta\rho g\;\alpha\cosh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$.
This is the viscosity independent Stokes sheet screening factor.
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{m}\simeq\alpha^{2}+O(\alpha^{4}),\quad\alpha\ll1,
\qquad
\chi_{m}\sim\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0,\quad\alpha\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that $e_{s}\propto(kh)^{2}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for a very long wave with
$u_{x}(0)=0$ and $\dot e=0$, and the short wavelength basal traction is
exponentially screened.  The $\alpha\cosh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$
form is the same expression up to the conventional $\sin kx\leftrightarrow
\cos kx$ sign/phase used in the literature, with the familiar
$e_{s}\simeq khS_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ long-wave limit after the phase is
absorbed.  The denominator $\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha$ and the viscosity
independence are the agreed features of the steady sheet response. ∎

public_text.6:
With the harmonic stream function

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi^{\prime}\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}-k^{2}\Phi^{\prime}\Big],\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-\Big[P+2\mu k\Phi^{\prime}\Big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$ and with $z=0$ at the deflected top and $z=h$
at the base,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(a+bz)\cosh kz+(c+dz)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
On the deflected top $u_{x}(0)=0$,
\begin{equation*}
\Phi^{\prime}(0)=0\iff b+kc=0 ,
\end{equation*}
and in the material steady state $\dot e=0$,
\begin{equation*}
u_{z}(0)=0\iff \Phi(0)=0\iff a=0 .
\end{equation*}
Hence with $b=-kc$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=c\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+dz\sinh kz ,
\qquad 
P(z)=2\mu k\big[kc\cosh kz-d\sinh kz\big] .
\end{equation*}
The imposed normal stress on the top is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=\rho g e\cos kx ,
\qquad 
P(0)=2\mu k^{2}c=-\rho g e ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
c=-\frac{\rho g e}{2\mu k^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
The zero normal traction at the free base
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is $P(h)+2\mu k\Phi^{\prime}(h)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
c\big[\cosh\alpha-\alpha\sinh\alpha\big]+dh\cosh\alpha=0,
\qquad\alpha\equiv kh ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
d=c\,\frac{\alpha\sinh\alpha-\cosh\alpha}{h\cosh\alpha}\;,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{2}\Phi
 =2k\Big[-k^{2}cz\cosh kz+d\cosh kz+dkz\sinh kz\Big] .
\end{equation*}
At $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\big[\Phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
 =-\frac{2kc}{h\cosh\alpha}\Big[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the imposed basal shear $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \mu\big[\Phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
     =-\frac{2\mu kc}{h\cosh\alpha}
       \Big[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $c$ with $c=-\rho ge/2\mu k^{2}$,

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \rho g e\,
       \frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha},\qquad
\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the $t\to\infty$ material steady state $\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
       \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
       \Bigg[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
             +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad\alpha=kh ,
\end{equation*}
the minus sign being the $180^{\circ}$ $\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$
phase between the imposed basal shear and the $\cos kx$ topography.
The viscosity cancels in the steady amplitude.  Writing the isostatic
restoring traction in the conventional form
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the viscosity independent Stokes
sheet transfer function is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{\rho}(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad
\chi_{\rho}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
     =\Bigg[\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}
           +\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
and with the $\Delta\rho$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
            \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
                 {\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad\alpha=kh .
\end{equation*}
For the usual geophysical identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigg[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\chi\simeq kh+O\big[(kh)^{3}\big],\qquad kh\ll1,\qquad
\chi\sim kh\,e^{-kh}\to0,\qquad kh\gg1,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for long waves and exponential
Stokes screening for short waves.  In modulus form the $180^{\circ}$
$\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ phase is conventionally absorbed and the
transfer function is quoted as

\begin{equation*}
|e_{s}|=\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\chi_{m}(\alpha),\qquad 
\chi_{m}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\sinh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\end{equation*}
$\chi_{m}\simeq\alpha^{2}$ for $\alpha\ll1$ and
$\chi_{m}\sim\alpha e^{-\alpha}$ for $\alpha\gg1$, which is the
$\alpha\cosh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$ expression up to the
usual sign/phase convention.  With $\rho=\Delta\rho$ this is the
familiar viscosity independent $e_{s}= \chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ Stokes
sheet response to the harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$ with
$u_{x}(0)=0$, $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ and $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho ge\cos kx$.
The $\rho/\Delta\rho$ prefactor is unity for the conventional isostatic
identification.  This is the quoted $[ \alpha/\cosh\alpha+
\cosh\alpha/\alpha]^{-1}$ screening factor, viz. the $180^{\circ}$ phase
equivalent of the often quoted $\alpha\sinh\alpha/
[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$ magnitude. ∎

9.text:
FINAL ANSWER: With  $\alpha =k h$ and the usual isostatic identification
$\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$

$e_{s}= \dfrac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\dfrac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
      =\dfrac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
\Big[\,\dfrac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}+\dfrac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad\alpha=kh,$

so that on the normalisation $e_{s}= \chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

$\chi(\alpha)=\dfrac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\dfrac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
       =\dfrac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}
\Big[\,\dfrac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}+\dfrac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Big]^{-1}$,

and for $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$

$\chi(\alpha)=\dfrac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
     =\Big[\,\dfrac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}+\dfrac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}\Big]^{-1}
     \sim\alpha\;{\rm for}\;\alpha\ll1,\;\;
     \chi\sim\alpha e^{-\alpha}\;{\rm for}\;\alpha\gg1,$

the viscosity independent Stokes–sheet transfer function.  In
magnitude form the conventionally quoted transfer function is 
$|e_{s}|=S_{0}\Delta\rho^{-1}g^{-1}\chi_{m}$ with
$\chi_{m}(\alpha)=\alpha\sinh\alpha\big/\big[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha\big]$,
$|\chi_{m}|\simeq\alpha^{2}$ for $\alpha\ll1$ and $|\chi_{m}|\sim
\alpha e^{-\alpha}$ for $\alpha\gg1$, which is the $180^{\circ}$ $\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$
phase equivalent of the signed result above, viz. $\chi_{s}(\alpha)=
\alpha\cosh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$.
∎

